{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778887173712,"sessionID":"ses_1d210d0abffe4GvF65ndgbKVzq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_lNBdwut3soB1Ajv7fEcPoWCW","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\n+title: \"Zero-wait load bypass\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark is load-heavy, and the current 5-stage pipe pays a full bubble\n+  on every load-use pair even when the data memory is zero-wait. Removing\n+  that unnecessary interlock should raise iterations per cycle without\n+  changing the ISA or the top-level RVFI contract.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a combinational MEM-to-EX bypass for load results so a dependent\n+  instruction can consume the loaded word in the next cycle instead of\n+  stalling behind the load. Keep the existing dmem backpressure stall\n+  path intact, but stop treating zero-wait load-use pairs as mandatory\n+  bubbles.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Expose the combinational load result and metadata needed for a same-cycle bypass.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Add a forwarding input that can source operands directly from the current MEM-stage load data.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Relax the load-use interlock when the zero-wait MEM bypass can satisfy the dependency.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the new MEM-stage bypass signals through the pipeline.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /