{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778887178497,"sessionID":"ses_1d210d0abffe4GvF65ndgbKVzq","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_QnfU13TvfzT8nmYS61USns9g","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\n2: title: \"Zero-wait load bypass\"\n3: category: micro_opt\n4: motivation: |\n5:   CoreMark is load-heavy, and the current 5-stage pipe pays a full bubble\n6:   on every load-use pair even when the data memory is zero-wait. Removing\n7:   that unnecessary interlock should raise iterations per cycle without\n8:   changing the ISA or the top-level RVFI contract.\n9: hypothesis: |\n10:   Add a combinational MEM-to-EX bypass for load results so a dependent\n11:   instruction can consume the loaded word in the next cycle instead of\n12:   stalling behind the load. Keep the existing dmem backpressure stall\n13:   path intact, but stop treating zero-wait load-use pairs as mandatory\n14:   bubbles.\n15: expected_impact:\n16:   fitness_delta_pct: 5\n17:   confidence: medium\n18: changes:\n19:   - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n20:     description: \"Expose the combinational load result and metadata needed for a same-cycle bypass.\"\n21:   - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n22:     description: \"Add a forwarding input that can source operands directly from the current MEM-stage load data.\"\n23:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n24:     description: \"Relax the load-use interlock when the zero-wait MEM bypass can satisfy the dependency.\"\n25:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n26:     description: \"Wire the new MEM-stage bypass signals through the pipeline.\"\n\n(End of file - total 26 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Zero-wait load bypass\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark is load-heavy, and the current 5-stage pipe pays a full bubble\n  on every load-use pair even when the data memory is zero-wait. Removing\n  that unnecessary interlock should raise iterations per cycle without\n  changing the ISA or the top-level RVFI contract.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a combinational MEM-to-EX bypass for load results so a dependent\n  instruction can consume the loaded word in the next cycle instead of\n  stalling behind the load. Keep the existing dmem backpressure stall\n  path intact, but stop treating zero-wait load-use pairs as mandatory\n  bubbles.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Expose the combinational load result and metadata needed for a same-cycle bypass.\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260515-001-r1s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778887178474,"end":1778887178491}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_039f1e9e94fbfc1b016a07aa0a56a4819ab6047903229e0778"}},"id":"prt_e2df038d60019K2f72MwT7rXcz","sessionID":"ses_1d210d0abffe4GvF65ndgbKVzq","messageID":"msg_e2df0273a001r06qi2SYnYV62x"}}
