": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the exact operand-use bits alongside the existing predecode metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the same operand-use classification from the validated opcode/funct table so IF and decode stay aligned.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the operand-use bits into ID/EX so downstream logic can rely on the same source-operand semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use bits to avoid false stalls on instructions that do not read rs2.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Source-read stall masks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both source register fields for every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read one\\nor both sources. That creates avoidable compare fanout and can inject false\\nbubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both source register fields for every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read one\\nor both sources. That creates avoidable compare fanout and can inject false\\nbubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits to the IF-stage predecode bundle,\\nthen gate `hazard_unit.sv`'s load-use compare logic with those bits so it\\nonly checks source registers that the current instruction actually reads.\\nThis should remove false stalls on OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL,\\nFENCE, and SYSTEM forms while slightly shrinking the hot hazard path, all\\nwithout changing RVFI, traps, or retirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-read flags to the predecode bundle so later stages can distinguish architecturally used vs unused rs fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` bits alongside the existing predecode metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate the load-use compare tree with the source-read flags so unused rs fields cannot trigger false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-read flags to the predecode bundle so later stages can distinguish architecturally used vs unused rs fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.s...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"One-hot decode classes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The hot decode path still rebuilds opcode/funct legality and instruction\\nclass information from the raw 32-bit instruction in ID. Hoisting that\\nclassification into fetch-time predecode should cut repeated case-tree\\nwork and narrow the control fanout seen by the rest of the pipe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The hot decode path still rebuilds opcode/funct legality and instruction\\nclass information from the raw 32-bit instruction in ID. Hoisting that\\nclassification into fetch-time predecode should cut repeated case-tree\\nwork and narrow the control fanout seen by the rest of the pipe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add compact one-hot instruction-class metadata to the IF-stage predecode\\nbundle, then have ID consume those cached classes when forming control and\\nhazard inputs instead of repeatedly re-decoding the same opcode/funct\\npatterns from `instr[31:0]`. Keep the decoder's legality rules unchanged,\\nbut make the common control path structurally narrower so EX and MEM see\\nless combinational decode work on every cycle.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the predecode and pipeline bundles with compact one-hot instruction-class metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the instruction-class metadata once at fetch time.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded classes when building control signals instead of re-decoding from the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep legality semantics identical while simplifying dispatch around the predecoded instruction class inputs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the predecode and pipeline bundles with compact one-hot instructi...","metadata":{"matches":17,"truncated":false},"title":"micro_opt|r10s0|forward_unit|id_stage|decoder|hazard|predecode","time":{"start":1778929899930,"end":1778929899991}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_00b06c03c4a5c5e5016a0850ea51e08199ad4dfd327bacb8d5"}},"id":"prt_e307c1990001LbI7GSSMPFGOoP","sessionID":"ses_1cf8402bdffejHwQXRomOTdHi7","messageID":"msg_e307c00e0001YZnyDYBaJlQQiT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778929900131,"sessionID":"ses_1cf8402bdffejHwQXRomOTdHi7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_bCulVTwFb0ZzTZsehTuYLRZP","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n11: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n12: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n13: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n14: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n15: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n16: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n17: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Gating RVFI `rs1`/`rs2` fields with operand-use bits changes the architectural retirement trace, so that optimization is only safe if the cosim/ISS side agrees on zeroing unused source registers.\n18: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r8s1 (improvement, +6.12%): Hoisting load/store lane decode into EX helps when MEM can consume aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign metadata directly, because it removes width-based recomputation from the hot memory path without changing RVFI or trap semantics.\n19: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny return stack only helps if its speculative fetch-side target stays synchronized with the architectural pipeline; otherwise canonical JALR return hints can desynchronize `pc_fwd` even when the predictor is return-only.\n20: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (regression, -7.54%): Hoisting one-hot instruction classes only pays when it removes enough work from the hot decode path; if ID still rechecks `funct3`/`funct7` and the wider predecode bundle adds fanout, the extra metadata can slow CoreMark instead of helping.\n\n(End of file - total 20 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Gating RVFI `rs1`/`rs2` fields with operand-use bits changes the architectural retirement trace, so that optimization is only safe if the cosim/ISS side agrees on zeroing unused source registers.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r8s1 (improvement, +6.12%): Hoisting load/store lane decode into EX helps when MEM can consume aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign metadata directly, because it removes width-based recomputation from the hot memory path without changing RVFI or trap semantics.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny return stack only helps if its speculative fetch-side target stays synchronized with the architectural pipeline; otherwise canonical JALR return hints can desynchronize `pc_fwd` even when the predictor is return-only.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (regression, -7.54%): Hoisting one-hot instruction classes only pays when it removes enough work from the hot decode path; if ID still rechecks `funct3`/`funct7` and the wider predecode bundle adds fanout, the extra metadata can slow CoreMark instead of helping.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778929899913,"end":1778929900125}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_00b06c03c4a5c5e5016a0850ea51d08199889556184a8abe0a"}},"id":"prt_e307c1950001hBHjSeyfgjgGRk","sessionID":"ses_1cf8402bdffejHwQXRomOTdHi7","messageID":"msg_e307c00e0001YZnyDYBaJlQQiT"}}
