{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778912314984,"sessionID":"ses_1d0910eb2ffeLcXI4S8noSxLSv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_uUrgUVtChgUrXOkcE89dXdSp","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\n+title: \"Operand-use compare gating\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\n+  source registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\n+  raw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\n+  and can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Predecode `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` once in IF, carry those bits through\n+  IF/ID and ID/EX, and use them to short-circuit the forwarding and\n+  load-use compare trees. This should trim always-on compare logic from the\n+  hottest control path while preserving RVFI, trap behavior, and in-order\n+  retirement.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add operand-use flags to the predecode and pipeline bundles so source-register activity is tracked explicitly.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Derive `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` from the fetched opcode class and latch them with the predecoded instruction.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Carry the operand-use flags into ID/EX alongside the decoded control bundle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use flags so instructions that do not read rs2 do not trigger false stalls.\"\n+  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Use the operand-use flags to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the EX-stage forwarding network.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\n+title: \"Operand-use compare gating\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\n+  source registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\n+  raw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\n+  and can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Predecode `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` once in IF, carry those bits through\n+  IF/ID and ID/EX, and use them to short-circuit the forwarding and\n+  load-use compare trees. This should trim always-on compare logic from the\n+  hottest control path while preserving RVFI, trap behavior, and in-order\n+  retirement.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add operand-use flags to the predecode and pipeline bundles so source-register activity is tracked explicitly.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Derive `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` from the fetched opcode class and latch them with the predecoded instruction.\"\n+  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Carry the operand-use flags into ID/EX alongside the decoded control bundle.\"\n+  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use flags so instructions that do not read rs2 do not trigger false stalls.\"\n+  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Use the operand-use flags to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the EX-stage forwarding network.\"\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml","relativePath":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml","type":"add","patch":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@\n+id: hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\n+title: \"Operand-use compare gating\"\n+category: micro_opt\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\n+  source registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\n+  raw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\n+  and can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Predecode `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` once in IF, carry those bits through\n+  IF/ID and ID/EX, and use them to short-circuit the forwarding and\n+  load-use compare trees. This should trim always-on compare logic from the\n+  hottest control path while preserving RVFI, trap behavior, and in-order\n+  retirement.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Add operand-use flags to the predecode and pipeline bundles so source-register activity is tracked explicitly.\"\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.s