{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778912318029,"sessionID":"ses_1d0910eb2ffeLcXI4S8noSxLSv","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_JcwKyTTgjViU7n8dcLiorHoC","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml","offset":1,"limit":200},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: id: hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\n2: title: \"Operand-use compare gating\"\n3: category: micro_opt\n4: motivation: |\n5:   CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\n6:   source registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\n7:   raw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\n8:   and can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\n9: hypothesis: |\n10:   Predecode `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` once in IF, carry those bits through\n11:   IF/ID and ID/EX, and use them to short-circuit the forwarding and\n12:   load-use compare trees. This should trim always-on compare logic from the\n13:   hottest control path while preserving RVFI, trap behavior, and in-order\n14:   retirement.\n15: expected_impact:\n16:   fitness_delta_pct: 4\n17:   confidence: medium\n18: changes:\n19:   - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n20:     description: \"Add operand-use flags to the predecode and pipeline bundles so source-register activity is tracked explicitly.\"\n21:   - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n22:     description: \"Derive `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` from the fetched opcode class and latch them with the predecoded instruction.\"\n23:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n24:     description: \"Carry the operand-use flags into ID/EX alongside the decoded control bundle.\"\n25:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n26:     description: \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use flags so instructions that do not read rs2 do not trigger false stalls.\"\n27:   - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n28:     description: \"Use the operand-use flags to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the EX-stage forwarding network.\"\n\n(End of file - total 28 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\ntitle: \"Operand-use compare gating\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\n  source registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\n  raw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\n  and can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predecode `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` once in IF, carry those bits through\n  IF/ID and ID/EX, and use them to short-circuit the forwarding and\n  load-use compare trees. This should trim always-on compare logic from the\n  hottest control path while preserving RVFI, trap behavior, and in-order\n  retirement.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add operand-use flags to the predecode and pipeline bundles so source-register activity is tracked explicitly.\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r7s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778912317985,"end":1778912318028}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0488bf3f7fe522ef016a080c3ddd008199845c03eb1124dd62"}},"id":"prt_e2f6fd20e001DbS5sMD7375nkl","sessionID":"ses_1d0910eb2ffeLcXI4S8noSxLSv","messageID":"msg_e2f6fc86c001v4gSQRBVrcg6sf"}}
