ned.\"\n25:   - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n26:     description: \"Carry the operand-use bits into ID/EX so downstream logic can rely on the same source-operand semantics.\"\n27:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n28:     description: \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use bits to avoid false stalls on instructions that do not read rs2.\"\n29:   - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n30:     description: \"Use the operand-use bits to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the forwarding network.\"\n\n(End of file - total 30 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260516-001-r8s0\ntitle: \"Decoder-aligned operand-use gating\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not actually read both\n  source registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares raw\n  rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot control path and can\n  also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits to the predecode and control path using\n  the same opcode/funct classification as the decoder, then gate load-use\n  detection and EX forwarding compares with those bits. That should remove\n  false stalls on single-source instructions and trim always-on compare logic\n  without changing RVFI, traps, or in-order retirement.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add source-operand-use fields to the predecode/control bundles and keep the classification shape shared across stages.\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-001-r8s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778919431273,"end":1778919431426}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0846baa93948def2016a082806dc10819aa67b6bf92f86e969"}},"id":"prt_e2fdc5bb3001qmjZDDNExj5vg0","sessionID":"ses_1d0264394ffehpd2FSJobtZhHW","messageID":"msg_e2fdc412b001k49hts6T58o6xJ"}}
