ifying it should trim a small but always-active hot path without\\nchanging architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The regfile currently spends combinational logic on same-cycle write-first\\nbypassing for both read ports. That path is redundant with the existing\\nEX-stage forwarding network, and it sits on every decode-cycle operand read.\\nSimplifying it should trim a small but always-active hot path without\\nchanging architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove the write-first bypass muxes from `reg_file.sv` and rely on the\\nexisting pipeline forwarding to supply younger values when an instruction\\nreaches EX. The ID stage still reads the register array combinationally, but\\nsame-cycle WB-to-ID visibility is no longer implemented inside the regfile,\\nwhich should reduce read-port fanout and improve Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Drop the same-cycle write-first read bypass so the read ports become simple array reads with only x0 special-casing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Drop the same-cycle write-first read bypass so the read ports become simple array reads with only x0 special-casing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.96, \"delta_pct\": -1.06, \"vs_baseline\": 9.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 138.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10266, \"ff\": 1898, \"seeds\": [132.36, 138.77, 143.35], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\"...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Predecode instruction slices\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current decode path repeatedly fans the full 32-bit instruction into\\ndecoder, imm_gen, and hazard logic. A narrower predecode record should cut\\ncombinational fanout and bit-slicing depth in the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path\\nwithout changing any architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current decode path repeatedly fans the full 32-bit instruction into\\ndecoder, imm_gen, and hazard logic. A narrower predecode record should cut\\ncombinational fanout and bit-slicing depth in the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path\\nwithout changing any architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact predecode bundle in IF that latches opcode, funct3/funct7,\\nrd/rs1/rs2, and the already-formed immediate fragments, then have ID use\\nthose narrow fields instead of re-slicing the raw instruction everywhere.\\nThis is a structural timing cleanup aimed at reducing the decode-critical\\npath and top-level instruction fanout while keeping RVFI, traps, and\\nretirement order unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact predecode bundle to carry opcode/funct and register slices through IF/ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture the predecode bundle alongside the fetched instruction so downstream logic sees narrow fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded slices instead of repeatedly extracting them from the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor decode to operate on predecoded opcode/funct inputs while keeping legality checks unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/imm_gen.sv\", \"description\": \"Optionally source immediate fragments from the predecode bundle to shorten decode fanout.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-use hazard gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The core already predecodes opcode and register slices, but the hazard\\nand forwarding logic still compares raw rs fields for every instruction.\\nThat creates avoidable compare fanout and can also stall on rs2 bits that\\nare really immediates in single-source instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core already predecodes opcode and register slices, but the hazard\\nand forwarding logic still compares raw rs fields for every instruction.\\nThat creates avoidable compare fanout and can also stall on rs2 bits that\\nare really immediates in single-source instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add predecoded operand-use flags so the pipe only compares rs1/rs2 when\\nthe current instruction actually reads them. Carry those flags through\\nIF/ID and ID/EX, then gate load-use detection and EX forwarding selects\\nwith them to remove spurious stalls and trim hot compare logic without\\nchanging RVFI or retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the pipeline bundles with operand-use bits for rs1/rs2 so downstream hazard and forwarding logic can ignore unused source fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode which source operands are actually read and carry those bits alongside the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the operand-use flags into ID/EX so execute-side forwarding can skip irrelevant source compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use checks with the operand-use flags to avoid false stalls on immediate and single-source instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the operand-use flags to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the EX-stage forwarding network.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"c...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv']\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is heavy on loads and stores, and `mem_stage.sv` still recomputes\\nbyte masks, address alignment, and load shift behavior from `alu_result`\\nand `mem_width` on every memory op. Moving that address-derived metadata\\nearlier should shorten the hot memory path and reduce fanout without\\nchanging any architectural results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add precomputed memory-lane metadata to the EX/MEM bundle: word-aligned\\naddress, byte mask, shift amount, and misalignment status. Have\\n`ex_stage.sv` derive those fields once from `alu_result[1:0]` and\\n`mem_width`, then let `mem_stage.sv` consume them directly so it only\\ngates the bus and forms the final load value. This is a structural\\ncleanup aimed at trimming the memory-access critical path while keeping\\nRVFI, traps, and retirement order unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata such as aligned address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalign flag.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive the memory-lane metadata once from the ALU result and load/store width before the MEM stage.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the precomputed lane metadata so the memory stage no longer repeats width-based mask, alignment, and shift logic.\"}]}, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup onl...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Advisory loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated loop backedges and direct jumps,\\nso the core keeps paying the EX-stage fetch bubble on control transfers\\nthat are cheap to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nis the lowest-risk way to hide more of that control-flow latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated loop backedges and direct jumps,\\nso the core keeps paying the EX-stage fetch bubble on control transfers\\nthat are cheap to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nis the lowest-risk way to hide more of that control-flow latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a purely combinational BTFNT-style hint in IF: predict direct JALs\\ntaken and predict conditional branches taken only when the immediate is\\nbackward, while forward branches fall through. Keep EX-stage resolution\\nas the architectural source of truth for redirects and mispredict repair,\\nso the predictor only changes speculative fetch and not RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction hint in fetch, but leave EX redirects and wrong-path squashing authoritative.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction hint in fetch, but leave EX redirects and wrong-path squashing authoritative.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 294.69, \"delta_pct\": -8.75, \"vs_baseline\": 4.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 125.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10478, \"ff\": 1929, \"seeds\": [127.75, 125.53, 122.74], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T06:16:34.091429Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson...\n  Line 20: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r7s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-use compare gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\\nraw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\\nand can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares\\nraw rs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot decode path\\nand can also create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Predecode `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` once in IF, carry those bits through\\nIF/ID and ID/EX, and use them to short-circuit the forwarding and\\nload-use compare trees. This should trim always-on compare logic from the\\nhottest control path while preserving RVFI, trap behavior, and in-order\\nretirement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add operand-use flags to the predecode and pipeline bundles so source-register activity is tracked explicitly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` from the fetched opcode class and latch them with the predecoded instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the operand-use flags into ID/EX alongside the decoded control bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use flags so instructions that do not read rs2 do not trigger false stalls.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the operand-use flags to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the EX-stage forwarding network.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"descript...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Richer predecode for decode fanout\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The last structural win came from shrinking the instruction fanout into\\ndecode. There is still more repeated opcode/funct classification on the hot\\nIF/ID -> ID/EX path, and that work can be hoisted earlier without changing\\nretirement or trap behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The last structural win came from shrinking the instruction fanout into\\ndecode. There is still more repeated opcode/funct classification on the hot\\nIF/ID -> ID/EX path, and that work can be hoisted earlier without changing\\nretirement or trap behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the IF-stage predecode bundle with compact instruction-class and\\nlegality bits so ID no longer rebuilds those classifications from the raw\\n32-bit instruction on every cycle. This should trim another slice of decode\\nfanout and case-tree depth while leaving the architectural control flow,\\nRVFI contract, and memory behavior unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact instruction-class / legality fields to the predecode and pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the richer predecode metadata once at fetch time and carry it forward with the instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded instruction-class bits instead of rebuilding opcode/funct legality from scratch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Simplify decode dispatch so it consumes the hoisted classification data without changing legality rules.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact instruction-class / legality fields to the predecode and pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the richer predecode metadata once ...\n  Line 23: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r8s0\", \"title\": \"Decoder-aligned operand-use gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not actually read both\\nsource registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares raw\\nrs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot control path and can\\nalso create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not actually read both\\nsource registers. The current hazard and forwarding logic still compares raw\\nrs fields unconditionally, which adds fanout on the hot control path and can\\nalso create avoidable load-use stalls on immediate-style ops.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits to the predecode and control path using\\nthe same opcode/funct classification as the decoder, then gate load-use\\ndetection and EX forwarding compares with those bits. That should remove\\nfalse stalls on single-source instructions and trim always-on compare logic\\nwithout changing RVFI, traps, or in-order retirement.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-operand-use fields to the predecode/control bundles and keep the classification shape shared across stages.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the exact operand-use bits alongside the existing predecode metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Populate the same operand-use classification from the validated opcode/funct table so IF and decode stay aligned.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Carry the operand-use bits into ID/EX so downstream logic can rely on the same source-operand semantics.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use detection with the operand-use bits to avoid false stalls on instructions that do not read rs2.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_...\n  Line 24: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r8s1\", \"title\": \"Move memory lane work earlier\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark is still heavy on loads and stores, and the current MEM stage\\nrecomputes byte masks, alignment, and shift selection from `alu_result`\\non every access. Hoisting that address-lane work earlier should shorten\\nthe hot memory path and reduce fanout without changing architectural\\nbehavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is still heavy on loads and stores, and the current MEM stage\\nrecomputes byte masks, alignment, and shift selection from `alu_result`\\non every access. Hoisting that address-lane work earlier should shorten\\nthe hot memory path and reduce fanout without changing architectural\\nbehavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata such as\\nthe aligned address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalign flag, then\\nhave `ex_stage.sv` derive those fields once from `alu_result` and\\n`mem_width` before `mem_stage.sv` consumes them directly. This keeps the\\nRVFI, trap, and retirement contract unchanged while trimming redundant\\nwidth-based logic from the memory-access critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata for address alignment, byte masking, and shift control.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive and latch the memory-lane metadata once from the ALU result and load/store width before the MEM stage.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the precomputed lane metadata so the memory stage no longer repeats width-based mask, alignment, and shift logic.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata for address alignment, byte masking, and shift control.\"...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Return-only stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays a repeatable bubble on function returns, and the prior\\nbroader predictor attempts were too expensive in IF. A very small return\\nstack can target that hot path without adding full BTB logic or changing\\nthe architectural redirect source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays a repeatable bubble on function returns, and the prior\\nbroader predictor attempts were too expensive in IF. A very small return\\nstack can target that hot path without adding full BTB logic or changing\\nthe architectural redirect source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack that predicts only canonical JALR returns.\\nUpdate it from already-latched PC+4 on resolved calls, use it speculatively\\nonly when the fetched instruction decodes as a return, and keep EX-stage\\nredirection authoritative for recovery. By narrowing the predictor to\\nreturns only, the design should stay much cheaper than the earlier\\ncall/return predictor while still removing recurring control-flow bubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the return-stack hint for speculative next-PC selection on canonical returns, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved call/return metadata and PC+4 into the predictor update path without changing RVFI or trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/return_stack.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack and canonical return-classification logic.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the return-stack hint for speculative next-PC selection on canonical returns, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolve...\n  Line 26: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r9s0\", \"title\": \"Source-read stall masks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both source register fields for every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read one\\nor both sources. That creates avoidable compare fanout and can inject false\\nbubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both source register fields for every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read one\\nor both sources. That creates avoidable compare fanout and can inject false\\nbubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits to the IF-stage predecode bundle,\\nthen gate `hazard_unit.sv`'s load-use compare logic with those bits so it\\nonly checks source registers that the current instruction actually reads.\\nThis should remove false stalls on OP-IMM, LOAD, JALR, LUI, AUIPC, JAL,\\nFENCE, and SYSTEM forms while slightly shrinking the hot hazard path, all\\nwithout changing RVFI, traps, or retirement order.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-read flags to the predecode bundle so later stages can distinguish architecturally used vs unused rs fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` bits alongside the existing predecode metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate the load-use compare tree with the source-read flags so unused rs fields cannot trigger false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add source-read flags to the predecode bundle so later stages can distinguish architecturally used vs unused rs fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.s...\n  Line 27: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r9s1\", \"title\": \"One-hot decode classes\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The hot decode path still rebuilds opcode/funct legality and instruction\\nclass information from the raw 32-bit instruction in ID. Hoisting that\\nclassification into fetch-time predecode should cut repeated case-tree\\nwork and narrow the control fanout seen by the rest of the pipe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The hot decode path still rebuilds opcode/funct legality and instruction\\nclass information from the raw 32-bit instruction in ID. Hoisting that\\nclassification into fetch-time predecode should cut repeated case-tree\\nwork and narrow the control fanout seen by the rest of the pipe.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add compact one-hot instruction-class metadata to the IF-stage predecode\\nbundle, then have ID consume those cached classes when forming control and\\nhazard inputs instead of repeatedly re-decoding the same opcode/funct\\npatterns from `instr[31:0]`. Keep the decoder's legality rules unchanged,\\nbut make the common control path structurally narrower so EX and MEM see\\nless combinational decode work on every cycle.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the predecode and pipeline bundles with compact one-hot instruction-class metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the instruction-class metadata once at fetch time.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded classes when building control signals instead of re-decoding from the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Keep legality semantics identical while simplifying dispatch around the predecoded instruction class inputs.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the predecode and pipeline bundles with compact one-hot instructi...","metadata":{"matches":23,"truncated":false},"title":"structural|fanout|bundle|predecode|forward|memory path|hot path","time":{"start":1778929905446,"end":1778929905628}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_05cc4eb07e1ba0df016a0850f145288199a9b17fc54d53085c"}},"id":"prt_e307c2f1f001qsZr5iz4u91qsm","sessionID":"ses_1cf840481ffeiqSKLxnF6h3aqX","messageID":"msg_e307bffe70015y5b33f1d0xDiF"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778929905699,"sessionID":"ses_1cf840481ffeiqSKLxnF6h3aqX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_TrLPKvPw57pD7FliboBG81hQ","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n11: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n12: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n13: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n14: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n15: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n16: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n17: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Gating RVFI `rs1`/`rs2` fields with operand-use bits changes the architectural retirement trace, so that optimization is only safe if the cosim/ISS side agrees on zeroing unused source registers.\n18: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r8s1 (improvement, +6.12%): Hoisting load/store lane decode into EX helps when MEM can consume aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign metadata directly, because it removes width-based recomputation from the hot memory path without changing RVFI or trap semantics.\n19: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny return stack only helps if its speculative fetch-side target stays synchronized with the architectural pipeline; otherwise canonical JALR return hints can desynchronize `pc_fwd` even when the predictor is return-only.\n20: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (regression, -7.54%): Hoisting one-hot instruction classes only pays when it removes enough work from the hot decode path; if ID still rechecks `funct3`/`funct7` and the wider predecode bundle adds fanout, the extra metadata can slow CoreMark instead of helping.\n\n(End of file - total 20 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Gating RVFI `rs1`/`rs2` fields with operand-use bits changes the architectural retirement trace, so that optimization is only safe if the cosim/ISS side agrees on zeroing unused source registers.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r8s1 (improvement, +6.12%): Hoisting load/store lane decode into EX helps when MEM can consume aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign metadata directly, because it removes width-based recomputation from the hot memory path without changing RVFI or trap semantics.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny return stack only helps if its speculative fetch-side target stays synchronized with the architectural pipeline; otherwise canonical JALR return hints can desynchronize `pc_fwd` even when the predictor is return-only.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (regression, -7.54%): Hoisting one-hot instruction classes only pays when it removes enough work from the hot decode path; if ID still rechecks `funct3`/`funct7` and the wider predecode bundle adds fanout, the extra metadata can slow CoreMark instead of helping.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778929905417,"end":1778929905697}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_05cc4eb07e1ba0df016a0850f1451481999efc280cfa103ebd"}},"id":"prt_e307c2ef3001bAZ3fkV736sVjS","sessionID":"ses_1cf840481ffeiqSKLxnF6h3aqX","messageID":"msg_e307bffe70015y5b33f1d0xDiF"}}
