{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778929979717,"sessionID":"ses_1cf840481ffeiqSKLxnF6h3aqX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_KE2aGpbE8fAVGqc2RrRlwTA1","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r10s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-002-r10s1\n+title: \"Split retire and writeback\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current MEM/WB boundary carries a lot of RVFI-only state through the\n+  same bundle that feeds the regfile and forwarding logic. That makes the\n+  hottest post-MEM path wider than it needs to be.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Split the wide retirement snapshot from the narrow writeback/bypass path:\n+  keep only rd, rd_wdata, reg_write, and valid on the signals used by the\n+  regfile and forwarding network, and carry the full RVFI snapshot on a\n+  separate sideband bundle. This should reduce fanout and routing pressure on\n+  the MEM/WB boundary without changing architectural retirement behavior.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Define separate narrow writeback and full retire-snapshot bundle types so the post-MEM path can be split cleanly.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the narrow writeback data separately from the full RVFI retirement snapshot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Consume the narrow writeback metadata instead of the full MEM/WB bundle for bypass comparisons.\"\n+  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Drive the regfile from the narrow writeback bundle with no dependency on RVFI-only fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire the split bundles so RVFI comes from the retire snapshot while writeback and forwarding use the narrow path.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the follo