{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778907920418,"sessionID":"ses_1d0d5ce71ffehNsS7Rr1i7GOzE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_zjgUPLWmclCBjFIpYE7ffRJy","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r6s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-002-r6s1\n+title: \"Hoist memory lane metadata\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark is heavy on loads and stores, and `mem_stage.sv` still recomputes\n+  byte masks, address alignment, and load shift behavior from `alu_result`\n+  and `mem_width` on every memory op. Moving that address-derived metadata\n+  earlier should shorten the hot memory path and reduce fanout without\n+  changing any architectural results.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add precomputed memory-lane metadata to the EX/MEM bundle: word-aligned\n+  address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalignment status. Have\n+  `ex_stage.sv` derive those fields once from `alu_result[1:0]` and\n+  `mem_width`, then let `mem_stage.sv` consume them directly so it only\n+  gates the bus and forms the final load value. This is a structural\n+  cleanup aimed at trimming the memory-access critical path while keeping\n+  RVFI, traps, and retirement order unchanged.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata such as aligned address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalign flag.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Derive the memory-lane metadata once from the ALU result and load/store width before the MEM stage.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Consume the precomputed lane metadata so the memory stage no longer repeats width-based mask, alignment, and shift logic.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r6s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r6s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r6s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-002-r6s1.yaml\n@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@\n+id: hyp-20260516-002-r6s1\n+title: \"Hoist memory lane metadata\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark is heavy on loads and stores, and `mem_stage.sv` still recomputes\n+  byte masks, address alignment, and load shift behavior from `alu_result`\n+  and `mem_width` on every memory op. Moving that address-derived metadata\n+  earlier should shorten the hot memory path and reduce fanout without\n+  changing any architectural results.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add precomputed memory-lane metadata to the EX/MEM bundle: word-aligned\n+  address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalignment status. Have\n+  `ex_stage.sv` derive those fields once from `alu_result[1:0]` and\n+  `mem_width`, then let `mem_stage.sv` consume them directly so it only\n+  gates the bus and forms the final load value. This is a structural\n+  cleanup aimed at trimming the memory-access critical path while keeping\n+  RVFI, traps, and retirement order unchanged.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata such as aligned address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalign flag.\"\n+  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Derive the memory-lane metadata once from the ALU result and load/store width before the MEM stage.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Consume the precomputed lane metadata so th