irect as\\nthe architectural source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small call/return predictor that speculatively redirects on\\ndirect JALs and uses a 4-entry return-address stack for canonical\\nJALR returns. Update the stack on retirement from already-latched PC+4\\nstate, keep EX-stage resolution authoritative for mispredict recovery,\\nand leave ordinary conditional branches on the existing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor's speculative next PC for calls and returns before EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retirement-time call/return update signals into the predictor and preserve EX redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/return_stack.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack and call/return classification logic.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor's speculative next PC for calls and returns before EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retirement-time call/return update signals into the predictor and preserve EX redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/return_stack.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny re...\n  Line 11: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r4s0\", \"title\": \"Latch writeback data in MEM\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"WB still rebuilds the final rd_wdata with a small mem_to_reg mux, and that\\nsignal fans out to both the regfile write port and the EX forwarding path.\\nHoisting the selection into MEM should shorten the common retire fanout\\nwithout changing architectural results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"WB still rebuilds the final rd_wdata with a small mem_to_reg mux, and that\\nsignal fans out to both the regfile write port and the EX forwarding path.\\nHoisting the selection into MEM should shorten the common retire fanout\\nwithout changing architectural results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a latched rd_wdata field to the MEM/WB bundle, compute it in\\nmem_stage.sv, and have wb_stage.sv forward that preselected value directly\\nto the regfile and bypass network. This keeps the ISA and RVFI behavior\\nunchanged while removing one more mux from the hot writeback path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the MEM/WB bundle with a latched rd_wdata field so writeback no longer has to remux ALU versus load data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the final writeback value alongside the existing load-data path and store it in the MEM/WB register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the final mem_to_reg mux with a direct pass-through of the preselected writeback value.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the MEM/WB bundle with a latched rd_wdata field so writeback no longer has to remux ALU versus load data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the final writeback value alongside the existing load-data path and store it in the MEM/WB register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Replace the final mem_to_reg mux w...\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist JALR target add\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"After hoisting branch targets and `pc+4` out of EX, the remaining hot\\ncontrol-flow arithmetic in the execute cone is the JALR `rs1 + imm`\\nadd. CoreMark still spends enough time on call/return paths that\\ntrimming that last adder should help Fmax without changing retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After hoisting branch targets and `pc+4` out of EX, the remaining hot\\ncontrol-flow arithmetic in the execute cone is the JALR `rs1 + imm`\\nadd. CoreMark still spends enough time on call/return paths that\\ntrimming that last adder should help Fmax without changing retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a latched `jalr_target` field to the ID/EX bundle and compute the\\nJALR destination in `id_stage.sv` from the decoded immediate plus a\\nforwarded rs1 value, then have `ex_stage.sv` use the precomputed target\\nfor redirect and misalignment checks. This keeps EX as the architectural\\nresolve point but removes the remaining JALR add/mux from its timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a latched `jalr_target` field to the ID/EX bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the forwarding information needed for an ID-side JALR target precompute.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute and latch the JALR target alongside the existing decode metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched JALR target and remove the EX-side `rs1 + imm` adder.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new JALR precompute path through the top-level pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a latched `jalr_target` field to the ID/EX bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the forwardi...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Advisory BTFNT fetch hint\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loops and repeated control\\ntransfers, so the core keeps paying the EX-stage branch bubble on paths\\nthat are easy to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nshould be cheap enough for this tiny FPGA core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loops and repeated control\\ntransfers, so the core keeps paying the EX-stage branch bubble on paths\\nthat are easy to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nshould be cheap enough for this tiny FPGA core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold a purely combinational BTFNT hint into IF: predict backward\\nconditional branches taken, forward conditional branches not taken, and\\ndirect JALs taken. Keep EX redirect as the architectural source of truth\\nso mispredict recovery stays simple and RVFI/trap behavior is unchanged,\\nbut hide more fetch bubbles on CoreMark's hot loops.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction predictor that chooses the speculative next PC while still yielding to EX-stage redirects.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction predictor that chooses the speculative next PC while still yielding to EX-stage redirects.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: insn_beq_ch0\\nsummary: counterexample trace: insn_blt_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY  0:18:39 [insn_blt_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_178_115 at rvfi_insn_check.sv:178.7-178.76 step 20\\nSBY  0:18:39 [insn_blt_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\nSBY  0:22:11 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ...\n  Line 14: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r5s0\", \"title\": \"Remove regfile write-first bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The regfile currently spends combinational logic on same-cycle write-first\\nbypassing for both read ports. That path is redundant with the existing\\nEX-stage forwarding network, and it sits on every decode-cycle operand read.\\nSimplifying it should trim a small but always-active hot path without\\nchanging architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The regfile currently spends combinational logic on same-cycle write-first\\nbypassing for both read ports. That path is redundant with the existing\\nEX-stage forwarding network, and it sits on every decode-cycle operand read.\\nSimplifying it should trim a small but always-active hot path without\\nchanging architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Remove the write-first bypass muxes from `reg_file.sv` and rely on the\\nexisting pipeline forwarding to supply younger values when an instruction\\nreaches EX. The ID stage still reads the register array combinationally, but\\nsame-cycle WB-to-ID visibility is no longer implemented inside the regfile,\\nwhich should reduce read-port fanout and improve Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Drop the same-cycle write-first read bypass so the read ports become simple array reads with only x0 special-casing.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/reg_file.sv\", \"description\": \"Drop the same-cycle write-first read bypass so the read ports become simple array reads with only x0 special-casing.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 308.96, \"delta_pct\": -1.06, \"vs_baseline\": 9.24, \"fmax_mhz\": 138.77, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10266, \"ff\": 1898, \"seeds\": [132.36, 138.77, 143.35], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\"...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Predecode instruction slices\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current decode path repeatedly fans the full 32-bit instruction into\\ndecoder, imm_gen, and hazard logic. A narrower predecode record should cut\\ncombinational fanout and bit-slicing depth in the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path\\nwithout changing any architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current decode path repeatedly fans the full 32-bit instruction into\\ndecoder, imm_gen, and hazard logic. A narrower predecode record should cut\\ncombinational fanout and bit-slicing depth in the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path\\nwithout changing any architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact predecode bundle in IF that latches opcode, funct3/funct7,\\nrd/rs1/rs2, and the already-formed immediate fragments, then have ID use\\nthose narrow fields instead of re-slicing the raw instruction everywhere.\\nThis is a structural timing cleanup aimed at reducing the decode-critical\\npath and top-level instruction fanout while keeping RVFI, traps, and\\nretirement order unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact predecode bundle to carry opcode/funct and register slices through IF/ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture the predecode bundle alongside the fetched instruction so downstream logic sees narrow fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded slices instead of repeatedly extracting them from the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor decode to operate on predecoded opcode/funct inputs while keeping legality checks unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/imm_gen.sv\", \"description\": \"Optionally source immediate fragments from the predecode bundle to shorten decode fanout.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl...\n  Line 17: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r6s0\", \"title\": \"Operand-use hazard gating\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The core already predecodes opcode and register slices, but the hazard\\nand forwarding logic still compares raw rs fields for every instruction.\\nThat creates avoidable compare fanout and can also stall on rs2 bits that\\nare really immediates in single-source instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The core already predecodes opcode and register slices, but the hazard\\nand forwarding logic still compares raw rs fields for every instruction.\\nThat creates avoidable compare fanout and can also stall on rs2 bits that\\nare really immediates in single-source instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add predecoded operand-use flags so the pipe only compares rs1/rs2 when\\nthe current instruction actually reads them. Carry those flags through\\nIF/ID and ID/EX, then gate load-use detection and EX forwarding selects\\nwith them to remove spurious stalls and trim hot compare logic without\\nchanging RVFI or retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the pipeline bundles with operand-use bits for rs1/rs2 so downstream hazard and forwarding logic can ignore unused source fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Predecode which source operands are actually read and carry those bits alongside the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the operand-use flags into ID/EX so execute-side forwarding can skip irrelevant source compares.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate load-use checks with the operand-use flags to avoid false stalls on immediate and single-source instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the operand-use flags to prune unnecessary rs compare logic in the EX-stage forwarding network.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"c...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv']\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is heavy on loads and stores, and `mem_stage.sv` still recomputes\\nbyte masks, address alignment, and load shift behavior from `alu_result`\\nand `mem_width` on every memory op. Moving that address-derived metadata\\nearlier should shorten the hot memory path and reduce fanout without\\nchanging any architectural results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add precomputed memory-lane metadata to the EX/MEM bundle: word-aligned\\naddress, byte mask, shift amount, and misalignment status. Have\\n`ex_stage.sv` derive those fields once from `alu_result[1:0]` and\\n`mem_width`, then let `mem_stage.sv` consume them directly so it only\\ngates the bus and forms the final load value. This is a structural\\ncleanup aimed at trimming the memory-access critical path while keeping\\nRVFI, traps, and retirement order unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata such as aligned address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalign flag.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive the memory-lane metadata once from the ALU result and load/store width before the MEM stage.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the precomputed lane metadata so the memory stage no longer repeats width-based mask, alignment, and shift logic.\"}]}, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup onl...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Advisory loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated loop backedges and direct jumps,\\nso the core keeps paying the EX-stage fetch bubble on control transfers\\nthat are cheap to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nis the lowest-risk way to hide more of that control-flow latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated loop backedges and direct jumps,\\nso the core keeps paying the EX-stage fetch bubble on control transfers\\nthat are cheap to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nis the lowest-risk way to hide more of that control-flow latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a purely combinational BTFNT-style hint in IF: predict direct JALs\\ntaken and predict conditional branches taken only when the immediate is\\nbackward, while forward branches fall through. Keep EX-stage resolution\\nas the architectural source of truth for redirects and mispredict repair,\\nso the predictor only changes speculative fetch and not RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction hint in fetch, but leave EX redirects and wrong-path squashing authoritative.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction hint in fetch, but leave EX redirects and wrong-path squashing authoritative.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 294.69, \"delta_pct\": -8.75, \"vs_baseline\": 4.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 125.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10478, \"ff\": 1929, \"seeds\": [127.75, 125.53, 122.74], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T06:16:34.091429Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson...","metadata":{"matches":17,"truncated":false},"title":"structural|predecode|bundle|fanout|retire|forward|hazard","time":{"start":1778912265151,"end":1778912265176}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0f348584c00be6fc016a080c08ea1481989c8f286b4d19283d"}},"id":"prt_e2f6f03b8001t5S0KdMqNTdwRR","sessionID":"ses_1d0910fb0ffeHm1SXpsYV2gloX","messageID":"msg_e2f6ef30b001oBMvytsCqVRsq4"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778912265185,"sessionID":"ses_1d0910fb0ffeHm1SXpsYV2gloX","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_GsKxmGDa2L79PjfuKrAPAxx8","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":300},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n11: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n12: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n13: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n14: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n15: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n\n(End of file - total 15 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-00