{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778919269959,"sessionID":"ses_1d02649b0ffeegxIcj9zf3QCr9","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_Wg36j41dDiUNofUZ99wBUy35","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"hyp-20260516-.*r[0-9]s[0-9].*structural|structural","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","include":"*.jsonl"},"output":"Found 7 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute branch targets in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes both the branch condition and the 32-bit\\nPC+imm target in the same cycle. Moving the target add upstream should\\nshorten the EX critical path and is a low-risk structural change for a\\nbranch-heavy CoreMark workload.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes both the branch condition and the 32-bit\\nPC+imm target in the same cycle. Moving the target add upstream should\\nshorten the EX critical path and is a low-risk structural change for a\\nbranch-heavy CoreMark workload.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a `branch_target` field to the ID/EX bundle, compute B-type targets\\nin `id_stage.sv`, and have `ex_stage.sv` reuse that precomputed target\\ninstead of redoing `pc + imm` in EX. This preserves the retirement and\\ntrap contract while trimming one adder from the execute-side timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched branch-target field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch targets during decode and store them in the pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched branch target and remove the EX-side branch-target adder.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched branch-target field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch targets during decode and store them in the pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched branch target and remove the EX-side branch-target adder.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 296.38, \"delta_pct\": 4.79, \"vs_baseline\": 4.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.12, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterat...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Latch pc+4 in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The execute stage still rebuilds the sequential fall-through PC in the hot\\nretirement path, even though that value is known as soon as the IF/ID PC is\\nlatched. Hoisting that adder out of EX should trim a little more logic from\\nthe branch/link critical path without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The execute stage still rebuilds the sequential fall-through PC in the hot\\nretirement path, even though that value is known as soon as the IF/ID PC is\\nlatched. Hoisting that adder out of EX should trim a little more logic from\\nthe branch/link critical path without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a latched `pc_plus4` field to the ID/EX bundle, compute it in\\n`id_stage.sv`, and have `ex_stage.sv` reuse that precomputed value for JAL\\nlink writeback and fall-through `pc_next` selection. This is a structural\\ntiming cleanup: it removes another 32-bit add from EX while preserving the\\nexisting control, RVFI, and trap contract.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched pc_plus4 field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and store pc_plus4 alongside the decoded instruction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse the latched pc_plus4 for JAL writeback and sequential next-PC selection.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched pc_plus4 field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and store pc_plus4 alongside the decoded instruction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse the latched pc_plus4 for JAL writeback and sequential next-PC selection.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 311.83, ...\n  Line 9: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r3s1\", \"title\": \"Reuse ID target for JAL\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"EX still rebuilds the PC-relative jump target for JAL even though the\\nsame PC+imm value is already available from ID. Reusing that latched\\ntarget should trim another 32-bit adder from the execute-side timing\\npath without changing retirement, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"EX still rebuilds the PC-relative jump target for JAL even though the\\nsame PC+imm value is already available from ID. Reusing that latched\\ntarget should trim another 32-bit adder from the execute-side timing\\npath without changing retirement, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Use the precomputed ID-stage branch target for JAL redirects and\\nmisalignment checks, and leave EX to compute only the rs1+imm target\\nneeded for JALR. This keeps control flow identical while removing one\\nmore hot add/mux pair from the EX critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse the latched PC-relative target for JAL so EX only computes the JALR target and associated trap/redirect bookkeeping.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Reuse the latched PC-relative target for JAL so EX only computes the JALR target and associated trap/redirect bookkeeping.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.65, \"delta_pct\": -0.38, \"vs_baseline\": 9.84, \"fmax_mhz\": 139.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10058, \"ff\": 1923, \"seeds\": [139.53, 141.16, 135.67], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T02:02:22.027157Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3}\n\n  Line 12: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r4s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist JALR target add\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"After hoisting branch targets and `pc+4` out of EX, the remaining hot\\ncontrol-flow arithmetic in the execute cone is the JALR `rs1 + imm`\\nadd. CoreMark still spends enough time on call/return paths that\\ntrimming that last adder should help Fmax without changing retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"After hoisting branch targets and `pc+4` out of EX, the remaining hot\\ncontrol-flow arithmetic in the execute cone is the JALR `rs1 + imm`\\nadd. CoreMark still spends enough time on call/return paths that\\ntrimming that last adder should help Fmax without changing retirement.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a latched `jalr_target` field to the ID/EX bundle and compute the\\nJALR destination in `id_stage.sv` from the decoded immediate plus a\\nforwarded rs1 value, then have `ex_stage.sv` use the precomputed target\\nfor redirect and misalignment checks. This keeps EX as the architectural\\nresolve point but removes the remaining JALR add/mux from its timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a latched `jalr_target` field to the ID/EX bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the forwarding information needed for an ID-side JALR target precompute.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute and latch the JALR target alongside the existing decode metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched JALR target and remove the EX-side `rs1 + imm` adder.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new JALR precompute path through the top-level pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a latched `jalr_target` field to the ID/EX bundle.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the forwardi...\n  Line 15: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r5s1\", \"title\": \"Predecode instruction slices\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current decode path repeatedly fans the full 32-bit instruction into\\ndecoder, imm_gen, and hazard logic. A narrower predecode record should cut\\ncombinational fanout and bit-slicing depth in the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path\\nwithout changing any architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current decode path repeatedly fans the full 32-bit instruction into\\ndecoder, imm_gen, and hazard logic. A narrower predecode record should cut\\ncombinational fanout and bit-slicing depth in the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path\\nwithout changing any architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact predecode bundle in IF that latches opcode, funct3/funct7,\\nrd/rs1/rs2, and the already-formed immediate fragments, then have ID use\\nthose narrow fields instead of re-slicing the raw instruction everywhere.\\nThis is a structural timing cleanup aimed at reducing the decode-critical\\npath and top-level instruction fanout while keeping RVFI, traps, and\\nretirement order unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a compact predecode bundle to carry opcode/funct and register slices through IF/ID.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Capture the predecode bundle alongside the fetched instruction so downstream logic sees narrow fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded slices instead of repeatedly extracting them from the full instruction word.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Refactor decode to operate on predecoded opcode/funct inputs while keeping legality checks unchanged.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/imm_gen.sv\", \"description\": \"Optionally source immediate fragments from the predecode bundle to shorten decode fanout.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl...\n  Line 18: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r6s1\", \"title\": \"(slot 1 hypothesis-gen failed)\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"hypothesis_gen_failed: Hypothesis agent modified off-limits paths and was rolled back: ['cores/bench/rtl/core.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/forward_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv', 'cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv']\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 6, \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is heavy on loads and stores, and `mem_stage.sv` still recomputes\\nbyte masks, address alignment, and load shift behavior from `alu_result`\\nand `mem_width` on every memory op. Moving that address-derived metadata\\nearlier should shorten the hot memory path and reduce fanout without\\nchanging any architectural results.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add precomputed memory-lane metadata to the EX/MEM bundle: word-aligned\\naddress, byte mask, shift amount, and misalignment status. Have\\n`ex_stage.sv` derive those fields once from `alu_result[1:0]` and\\n`mem_width`, then let `mem_stage.sv` consume them directly so it only\\ngates the bus and forms the final load value. This is a structural\\ncleanup aimed at trimming the memory-access critical path while keeping\\nRVFI, traps, and retirement order unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the EX/MEM bundle with precomputed memory-lane metadata such as aligned address, byte mask, shift amount, and misalign flag.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Derive the memory-lane metadata once from the ALU result and load/store width before the MEM stage.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the precomputed lane metadata so the memory stage no longer repeats width-based mask, alignment, and shift logic.\"}]}, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup onl...\n  Line 21: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r7s1\", \"title\": \"Richer predecode for decode fanout\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The last structural win came from shrinking the instruction fanout into\\ndecode. There is still more repeated opcode/funct classification on the hot\\nIF/ID -> ID/EX path, and that work can be hoisted earlier without changing\\nretirement or trap behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The last structural win came from shrinking the instruction fanout into\\ndecode. There is still more repeated opcode/funct classification on the hot\\nIF/ID -> ID/EX path, and that work can be hoisted earlier without changing\\nretirement or trap behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the IF-stage predecode bundle with compact instruction-class and\\nlegality bits so ID no longer rebuilds those classifications from the raw\\n32-bit instruction on every cycle. This should trim another slice of decode\\nfanout and case-tree depth while leaving the architectural control flow,\\nRVFI contract, and memory behavior unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact instruction-class / legality fields to the predecode and pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the richer predecode metadata once at fetch time and carry it forward with the instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the predecoded instruction-class bits instead of rebuilding opcode/funct legality from scratch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/decoder.sv\", \"description\": \"Simplify decode dispatch so it consumes the hoisted classification data without changing legality rules.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add compact instruction-class / legality fields to the predecode and pipeline bundles.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute the richer predecode metadata once ...","metadata":{"matches":7,"truncated":false},"title":"hyp-20260516-.*r[0-9]s[0-9].*structural|structural","time":{"start":1778919269669,"end":1778919269957}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_03c7585df5c52fa7016a082762cb4081998abb47fdcb3b75b7"}},"id":"prt_e2fd9e5100015O5CwFuUeLgtGE","sessionID":"ses_1d02649b0ffeegxIcj9zf3QCr9","messageID":"msg_e2fd9bb32001aIO9qSB5c5vAYT"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778919269980,"sessionID":"ses_1d02649b0ffeegxIcj9zf3QCr9","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_5f3wnxsKsC0a1u8XmhA8NRT3","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":260},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n11: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n12: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n13: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n14: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n15: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n16: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n\n(End of file - total 16 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before