{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778919278437,"sessionID":"ses_1d02649b0ffeegxIcj9zf3QCr9","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_shWye01RJDVlb3HVG8kd1yDu","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"\"category\": \"structural\"|memory-path|mem_stage|forward_unit|hazard_unit|predecode","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","include":"*.jsonl"},"output":"Found 12 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 2: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Zero-wait load bypass\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark is load-heavy, and the current 5-stage pipe pays a full bubble\\non every load-use pair even when the data memory is zero-wait. Removing\\nthat unnecessary interlock should raise iterations per cycle without\\nchanging the ISA or the top-level RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark is load-heavy, and the current 5-stage pipe pays a full bubble\\non every load-use pair even when the data memory is zero-wait. Removing\\nthat unnecessary interlock should raise iterations per cycle without\\nchanging the ISA or the top-level RVFI contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a combinational MEM-to-EX bypass for load results so a dependent\\ninstruction can consume the loaded word in the next cycle instead of\\nstalling behind the load. Keep the existing dmem backpressure stall\\npath intact, but stop treating zero-wait load-use pairs as mandatory\\nbubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinational load result and metadata needed for a same-cycle bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a forwarding input that can source operands directly from the current MEM-stage load data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Relax the load-use interlock when the zero-wait MEM bypass can satisfy the dependency.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new MEM-stage bypass signals through the pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose the combinational load result and metadata needed for a same-cycle bypass.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a forwarding input that can source operands directly from the current MEM-stage load data.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Relax the load-use inter...\n  Line 3: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Precompute branch targets in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes both the branch condition and the 32-bit\\nPC+imm target in the same cycle. Moving the target add upstream should\\nshorten the EX critical path and is a low-risk structural change for a\\nbranch-heavy CoreMark workload.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current EX stage computes both the branch condition and the 32-bit\\nPC+imm target in the same cycle. Moving the target add upstream should\\nshorten the EX critical path and is a low-risk structural change for a\\nbranch-heavy CoreMark workload.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a `branch_target` field to the ID/EX bundle, compute B-type targets\\nin `id_stage.sv`, and have `ex_stage.sv` reuse that precomputed target\\ninstead of redoing `pc + imm` in EX. This preserves the retirement and\\ntrap contract while trimming one adder from the execute-side timing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched branch-target field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch targets during decode and store them in the pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched branch target and remove the EX-side branch-target adder.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched branch-target field.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Precompute branch targets during decode and store them in the pipeline register.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched branch target and remove the EX-side branch-target adder.\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 296.38, \"delta_pct\": 4.79, \"vs_baseline\": 4.79, \"fmax_mhz\": 133.12, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterat...\n  Line 5: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-001-r2s0\", \"title\": \"Resolve branches in ID\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"Branch-heavy CoreMark spends a lot of time paying the taken-branch\\nbubble created by waiting until EX to know the outcome. Moving the\\ncommon conditional-branch decision into ID should cut one wrong-path\\ncycle while also shortening the EX timing cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"Branch-heavy CoreMark spends a lot of time paying the taken-branch\\nbubble created by waiting until EX to know the outcome. Moving the\\ncommon conditional-branch decision into ID should cut one wrong-path\\ncycle while also shortening the EX timing cone.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add local operand forwarding into ID so BEQ/BNE/BLT/BGE/BLTU/BGEU can\\nbe resolved as soon as decode has the operands, using the branch target\\nalready precomputed in ID. Keep EX as the architectural fallback for\\nthe remaining jump/misalign cases, but stop burning a full EX-stage\\nbubble on ordinary taken branches.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the pipeline bundle with any extra ID-side branch resolve state needed for early branch handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add branch compare logic and local forwarding so common conditional branches can resolve in decode.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Teach the hazard logic when an ID-resolved branch still needs a stall versus when it can redirect immediately.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new ID-stage branch decision into fetch/redirect control without changing the RVFI contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the pipeline bundle with any extra ID-side branch resolve state needed for early branch handling.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add branch compare logic and local forwarding...\n  Line 6: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-002-r2s1\", \"title\": \"Latch pc+4 in ID\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The execute stage still rebuilds the sequential fall-through PC in the hot\\nretirement path, even though that value is known as soon as the IF/ID PC is\\nlatched. Hoisting that adder out of EX should trim a little more logic from\\nthe branch/link critical path without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The execute stage still rebuilds the sequential fall-through PC in the hot\\nretirement path, even though that value is known as soon as the IF/ID PC is\\nlatched. Hoisting that adder out of EX should trim a little more logic from\\nthe branch/link critical path without changing architectural behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a latched `pc_plus4` field to the ID/EX bundle, compute it in\\n`id_stage.sv`, and have `ex_stage.sv` reuse that precomputed value for JAL\\nlink writeback and fall-through `pc_next` selection. This is a structural