{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778929978886,"sessionID":"ses_1cf840575ffex6HenLtj6HvPlw","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_9Lm7VMeCGqdP0AiFwSWw9Pl7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r10s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-003-r10s2\n+title: \"Narrow loop-backedge predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\n+  broader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\n+  or formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\n+  recent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\n+  while still removing a common fetch bubble.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny one-entry loop-backedge predictor in IF that remembers the last\n+  taken conditional branch PC, its target, and a single taken bit. Speculate\n+  only on exact PC hits for backward conditional branches, update only from\n+  retired conditional branches, and keep EX-stage redirect authoritative for\n+  recovery. This narrows the predictor enough to avoid the fanout and control\n+  complexity that hurt earlier predictor ideas while still targeting CoreMark's\n+  dominant loop pattern.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Use the loop-backedge hint to choose the speculative next PC on exact-hit backward branches, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"\n+  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n+    description: \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor with one-bit taken state, exact PC tagging, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA