{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778933647440,"sessionID":"ses_1cf4ad38fffet6KoTg5NSa2pJh","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_u5jw3tAaANHF44bCVUJW9cMg","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"predictor","path":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","include":"*.jsonl"},"output":"Found 10 matches\n/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl:\n  Line 4: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r1s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny BTB loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in short loops and repeated call/return\\npaths, so this core is paying the full EX-stage branch penalty on the\\nsame control transfers over and over. A very small predictor should\\nremove those bubbles with limited area cost and without changing the\\nin-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in short loops and repeated call/return\\npaths, so this core is paying the full EX-stage branch penalty on the\\nsame control transfers over and over. A very small predictor should\\nremove those bubbles with limited area cost and without changing the\\nin-order retirement contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a compact direct-mapped branch target buffer with 2-bit saturating\\ncounters and use it to speculatively redirect fetch on hot taken\\nbranches and jump targets from IF. Keep EX-stage resolution as the\\narchitectural source of truth so mispredict recovery stays simple and\\nthe RVFI / trap behavior remains unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread speculative next-PC selection through fetch and fall back to the existing redirect path on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor update path using EX-stage resolution signals.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small direct-mapped BTB with 2-bit counters and update logic for taken branches and jumps.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 6, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Thread speculative next-PC selection through fetch and fall back to the existing redirect path on mispredicts.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Instantiate and connect the predictor update path using EX-stage resolution signals.\"}, {\"fi...\n  Line 7: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r2s2\", \"title\": \"Static BTFNT predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in short loops, so the current EX-resolved\\nbranch path still pays avoidable fetch bubbles on repeated taken branches.\\nA predictor that redirects IF earlier, but keeps EX as the source of truth,\\ncan improve CoreMark/MHz without changing retirement or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time in short loops, so the current EX-resolved\\nbranch path still pays avoidable fetch bubbles on repeated taken branches.\\nA predictor that redirects IF earlier, but keeps EX as the source of truth,\\ncan improve CoreMark/MHz without changing retirement or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace the failed stateful BTB idea with a fully combinational static\\npredictor in IF: predict backward conditional branches taken, forward\\nconditional branches not taken, and always predict JALs taken from the\\nfetched instruction. This keeps the predictor update path trivial, avoids\\nspeculative training state, and should hide more of the branch penalty on\\nCoreMark's hot loops while preserving the existing mispredict redirect.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a static predictor to choose the speculative next PC before EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small combinational BTFNT predictor that decodes the fetched instruction and emits the predicted next PC.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a static predictor to choose the speculative next PC before EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a small combinational BTFNT predictor that decodes the fetched instruction and emits the predicted next PC.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_...\n  Line 10: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r3s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny call/return predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays a full fetch bubble on repeated call/return\\ncontrol flow, even after the branch-path timing cleanups. A tiny\\npredictor that handles calls and returns without general BTB state\\nshould improve the hot path while keeping the existing EX redirect as\\nthe architectural source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays a full fetch bubble on repeated call/return\\ncontrol flow, even after the branch-path timing cleanups. A tiny\\npredictor that handles calls and returns without general BTB state\\nshould improve the hot path while keeping the existing EX redirect as\\nthe architectural source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a small call/return predictor that speculatively redirects on\\ndirect JALs and uses a 4-entry return-address stack for canonical\\nJALR returns. Update the stack on retirement from already-latched PC+4\\nstate, keep EX-stage resolution authoritative for mispredict recovery,\\nand leave ordinary conditional branches on the existing path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor's speculative next PC for calls and returns before EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retirement-time call/return update signals into the predictor and preserve EX redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/return_stack.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack and call/return classification logic.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the predictor's speculative next PC for calls and returns before EX resolution.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retirement-time call/return update signals into the predictor and preserve EX redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/return_stack.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny re...\n  Line 13: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260515-003-r4s2\", \"title\": \"Advisory BTFNT fetch hint\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loops and repeated control\\ntransfers, so the core keeps paying the EX-stage branch bubble on paths\\nthat are easy to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nshould be cheap enough for this tiny FPGA core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time in tight loops and repeated control\\ntransfers, so the core keeps paying the EX-stage branch bubble on paths\\nthat are easy to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nshould be cheap enough for this tiny FPGA core.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Fold a purely combinational BTFNT hint into IF: predict backward\\nconditional branches taken, forward conditional branches not taken, and\\ndirect JALs taken. Keep EX redirect as the architectural source of truth\\nso mispredict recovery stays simple and RVFI/trap behavior is unchanged,\\nbut hide more fetch bubbles on CoreMark's hot loops.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction predictor that chooses the speculative next PC while still yielding to EX-stage redirects.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction predictor that chooses the speculative next PC while still yielding to EX-stage redirects.\"}], \"outcome\": \"broken\", \"formal_passed\": false, \"cosim_passed\": false, \"error\": \"formal_failed: insn_beq_ch0\\nsummary: counterexample trace: insn_blt_ch0/engine_0/trace.vcd\\nSBY  0:18:39 [insn_blt_ch0] summary:   failed assertion rvfi_testbench._witness_.check_flatten_checker_inst__assert_rvfi_insn_check_sv_178_115 at rvfi_insn_check.sv:178.7-178.76 step 20\\nSBY  0:18:39 [insn_blt_ch0] DONE (FAIL, rc=0)\\nSBY  0:22:11 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ...\n  Line 16: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r5s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny branch-direction predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles waiting on repeated control transfers, especially\\nshort loop backedges and recurring direct jumps. A very small predictor that\\nlearns branch direction without general BTB state could hide those bubbles\\nwhile keeping the existing EX redirect path as the architectural source of\\ntruth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles waiting on repeated control transfers, especially\\nshort loop backedges and recurring direct jumps. A very small predictor that\\nlearns branch direction without general BTB state could hide those bubbles\\nwhile keeping the existing EX redirect path as the architectural source of\\ntruth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped direction predictor in IF that speculatively chooses\\nbetween fall-through and the already-available control-flow target, while EX\\nresolution remains authoritative for recovery. Keep the state to a few\\nlearned taken/not-taken bits so repeated CoreMark branches can settle quickly\\nwithout the formal and timing cost of a full BTB or return-stack design.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use predictor output to select the speculative next PC before EX resolution, but keep EX redirects as the final authority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing RVFI or trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped branch-direction table with simple update logic and a conservative fallback to fall-through.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use predictor output to select the speculative next PC before EX resolution, but keep EX redirects as the ...\n  Line 19: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r6s2\", \"title\": \"Advisory loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated loop backedges and direct jumps,\\nso the core keeps paying the EX-stage fetch bubble on control transfers\\nthat are cheap to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nis the lowest-risk way to hide more of that control-flow latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends cycles on repeated loop backedges and direct jumps,\\nso the core keeps paying the EX-stage fetch bubble on control transfers\\nthat are cheap to predict statically. A predictor with no training state\\nis the lowest-risk way to hide more of that control-flow latency.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a purely combinational BTFNT-style hint in IF: predict direct JALs\\ntaken and predict conditional branches taken only when the immediate is\\nbackward, while forward branches fall through. Keep EX-stage resolution\\nas the architectural source of truth for redirects and mispredict repair,\\nso the predictor only changes speculative fetch and not RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction hint in fetch, but leave EX redirects and wrong-path squashing authoritative.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a stateless static branch-direction hint in fetch, but leave EX redirects and wrong-path squashing authoritative.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 294.69, \"delta_pct\": -8.75, \"vs_baseline\": 4.2, \"fmax_mhz\": 125.53, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4259699, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 10478, \"ff\": 1929, \"seeds\": [127.75, 125.53, 122.74], \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-05-16T06:16:34.091429Z\", \"slot\": 2, \"round_id\": 6, \"lesson...\n  Line 22: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r7s2\", \"title\": \"Single-entry loop predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark has hot loop backedges that repeat many times, but the prior\\nbroader predictors paid too much cost in IF logic and formal complexity.\\nA one-entry loop cache should capture the dominant repeat without the\\ntraining and state overhead of a general BTB or return stack.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark has hot loop backedges that repeat many times, but the prior\\nbroader predictors paid too much cost in IF logic and formal complexity.\\nA one-entry loop cache should capture the dominant repeat without the\\ntraining and state overhead of a general BTB or return stack.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry loop predictor in IF that remembers the last taken\\nconditional backedge's PC and target, and speculatively redirects only on\\na tag hit. Train it only from resolved EX outcomes, keep EX redirect\\nauthoritative on every miss or mismatch, and avoid speculative training\\nstate beyond that one cached loop target.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume a tiny loop-predictor hint for speculative next-PC selection, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved branch outcomes into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single-entry tagged loop-target cache with conservative fall-through on miss and update-on-resolved-branch behavior.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume a tiny loop-predictor hint for speculative next-PC selection, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved branch outcomes into the predictor update path without changing the RV...\n  Line 25: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r8s2\", \"title\": \"Return-only stack predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays a repeatable bubble on function returns, and the prior\\nbroader predictor attempts were too expensive in IF. A very small return\\nstack can target that hot path without adding full BTB logic or changing\\nthe architectural redirect source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still pays a repeatable bubble on function returns, and the prior\\nbroader predictor attempts were too expensive in IF. A very small return\\nstack can target that hot path without adding full BTB logic or changing\\nthe architectural redirect source of truth.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack that predicts only canonical JALR returns.\\nUpdate it from already-latched PC+4 on resolved calls, use it speculatively\\nonly when the fetched instruction decodes as a return, and keep EX-stage\\nredirection authoritative for recovery. By narrowing the predictor to\\nreturns only, the design should stay much cheaper than the earlier\\ncall/return predictor while still removing recurring control-flow bubbles.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the return-stack hint for speculative next-PC selection on canonical returns, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolved call/return metadata and PC+4 into the predictor update path without changing RVFI or trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/return_stack.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny return-address stack and canonical return-classification logic.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the return-stack hint for speculative next-PC selection on canonical returns, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire resolve...\n  Line 28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny bimodal branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\\nconditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\\nand stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\\nso a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\\nconditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\\nand stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\\nso a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped bimodal predictor that stores only 2-bit taken\\nconfidence for conditional branches, with the existing IF-stage immediate\\ndecode used to choose between fall-through and the predicted branch target.\\nUpdate the table only from retired conditional branches, keep EX-stage\\nresolution authoritative for recovery, and avoid BTB or return-stack\\ntarget storage entirely so the predictor stays cheap enough for this FPGA.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs the decoded branch target before EX resolves the branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped 2-bit branch-direction table with conservative fall-through defaults and retirement-time updates.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs...\n  Line 31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Narrow loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\\nor formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\\nrecent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\\nwhile still removing a common fetch bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\\nor formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\\nrecent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\\nwhile still removing a common fetch bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny one-entry loop-backedge predictor in IF that remembers the last\\ntaken conditional branch PC, its target, and a single taken bit. Speculate\\nonly on exact PC hits for backward conditional branches, update only from\\nretired conditional branches, and keep EX-stage redirect authoritative for\\nrecovery. This narrows the predictor enough to avoid the fanout and control\\ncomplexity that hurt earlier predictor ideas while still targeting CoreMark's\\ndominant loop pattern.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the loop-backedge hint to choose the speculative next PC on exact-hit backward branches, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor with one-bit taken state, exact PC tagging, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"conf...","metadata":{"matches":10,"truncated":false},"title":"predictor","time":{"start":1778933647325,"end":1778933647438}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0b8645a0eb9a8243016a085f8f1994819bb22a1c97ff524c17"}},"id":"prt_e30b547bf001RecCGaa5CJGPqB","sessionID":"ses_1cf4ad38fffet6KoTg5NSa2pJh","messageID":"msg_e30b53214001zLXHPUE8Tf3h1c"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778933647596,"sessionID":"ses_1cf4ad38fffet6KoTg5NSa2pJh","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_7UMwk9iyaTGOPW7lXTOkSv65","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md","offset":1,"limit":400},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/LESSONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n2: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n3: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n4: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n5: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n6: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n7: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n8: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n9: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n10: - 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n11: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n12: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n13: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n14: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n15: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n16: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n17: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Gating RVFI `rs1`/`rs2` fields with operand-use bits changes the architectural retirement trace, so that optimization is only safe if the cosim/ISS side agrees on zeroing unused source registers.\n18: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r8s1 (improvement, +6.12%): Hoisting load/store lane decode into EX helps when MEM can consume aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign metadata directly, because it removes width-based recomputation from the hot memory path without changing RVFI or trap semantics.\n19: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny return stack only helps if its speculative fetch-side target stays synchronized with the architectural pipeline; otherwise canonical JALR return hints can desynchronize `pc_fwd` even when the predictor is return-only.\n20: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (regression, -7.54%): Hoisting one-hot instruction classes only pays when it removes enough work from the hot decode path; if ID still rechecks `funct3`/`funct7` and the wider predecode bundle adds fanout, the extra metadata can slow CoreMark instead of helping.\n21: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r10s0 (broken, n/a): Decode-time `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` hints are only safe as a replacement for raw load-use compares if they are proven conservative for every opcode class; one missed dependency turns a stall optimization into a real retirement error that `reg` formal will catch.\n22: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r10s1 (regression, -10.79%): Splitting RVFI metadata into a sideband only helps if that wide retirement snapshot is truly off the hot MEM/WB boundary; if the core still has to latch and route both `wb_t` and `retire_t` there, the extra plumbing can outweigh the narrower bypass path.\n23: - 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r10s2 (broken, n/a): A one-entry loop-backedge predictor only stays safe if lookup and training are anchored to retired branch state; once IF starts speculating from live fetch PC/opcode data, `pc_fwd` can fail even though EX redirect still owns architectural recovery.\n\n(End of file - total 23 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r1s0 (broken, n/a): Zero-wait MEM-to-EX load bypasses must stay coupled to a real `dmem_ready`/`load_valid` handshake and RVFI-consistent writeback, or the dependent operand can observe an uncommitted load and fail `reg` formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r1s1 (improvement, +4.79%): Precomputing branch targets in ID lets EX reuse the target for redirect and misalignment checks, trimming a branch-path adder without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r1s2 (broken, n/a): Even a tiny BTB can make formal time out if predictor state is threaded through IF/EX redirect and update paths too tightly; keep speculative training simple or the proof search space can explode before functional bugs are exposed.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r2s0 (broken, n/a): Moving branch decisions into ID must be paired with complete operand forwarding/stalling and consistent redirect bookkeeping, or stale compares will trip branch, PC, and reg-order formal checks.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r2s1 (improvement, +5.21%): Latching `pc+4` in ID pays off because JAL link writeback and fall-through redirect both need the same sequential PC, so reusing it in EX removes a hot 32-bit add from the retirement path without changing control flow.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r2s2 (broken, n/a): A combinational BTFNT predictor can only hide fetch bubbles if EX redirect still wins the final PC mux; letting IF prediction own `next_pc` breaks branch correctness on taken paths because the redirect target no longer has priority.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r3s2 (regression, -30.72%): A tiny call/return predictor only pays off if it resolves often enough to erase the fetch bubble; threading `pred_next_pc` and return-stack updates through retirement/recovery just adds control logic when the benchmark's control flow is still dominated by the simple EX redirect path.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-001-r4s0 (improvement, +0.14%): Latching the final `rd_wdata` in MEM helps because both the regfile write port and EX forwarding consume the same selected value, so hoisting the `mem_to_reg` choice off the WB hot path removes a small fanout mux without changing architectural state.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-002-r4s1 (broken, n/a): Hoisting JALR target add into ID only works if the new `rs1` forwarding path stays in lockstep with the architectural register-flow checks; otherwise the core can redirect on a precomputed address while `riscv-formal` still catches a mismatch in the retired source-register trace.\n- 2026-05-15 hyp-20260515-003-r4s2 (broken, n/a): A BTFNT hint must stay purely advisory; once IF recomputes `next_pc` from the fetched opcode/immediate, branch formal checks start depending on speculative fetch state instead of the EX-resolved architectural branch decision.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r5s0 (regression, -1.06%): Moving same-cycle WB visibility out of `reg_file.sv` only helps if it also leaves the decode operand-read path shorter; reintroducing the compare/mux in `id_stage.sv` keeps the hot ID critical path active and can erase the expected Fmax win.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r5s1 (improvement, +3.42%): Predecoding opcode/funct/rs fields and immediates once in IF pays off because ID and hazard logic can consume a narrow bundle instead of repeatedly slicing `instr[31:0]`, which trims the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX fanout path without changing retirement behavior.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r5s2 (broken, n/a): A direction predictor must keep wrong-path squashing tied to EX resolution, not `if_id_pc == redirect_target`, because fetch-PC equality can suppress a needed flush and let speculative IF/ID state break branch formal checks.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r6s1 (broken, n/a): Structural hot-path cleanup only works when the new bundle fields are added in the allowed pipeline boundary; if the refactor spills into top-level or unrelated decode/hazard modules, the hypothesis gets rolled back before the memory-path benefit can be measured.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.75%): A stateless BTFNT predictor only helps if its IF-side immediate decode and target selection stay cheaper than the fetch bubbles it removes; on CoreMark, that extra speculative PC logic outweighed the loop-backedge wins.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r7s0 (broken, n/a): Predecoded `uses_rs1`/`uses_rs2` bits can gate forwarding and load-use compares safely only if every stage shares the same opcode-to-operand-read semantics; otherwise the bypass/stall logic drifts from architectural operand flow and cosim will fail.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-001-r8s0 (broken, n/a): Gating RVFI `rs1`/`rs2` fields with operand-use bits changes the architectural retirement trace, so that optimization is only safe if the cosim/ISS side agrees on zeroing unused source registers.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r8s1 (improvement, +6.12%): Hoisting load/store lane decode into EX helps when MEM can consume aligned address, byte mask, shift, and misalign metadata directly, because it removes width-based recomputation from the hot memory path without changing RVFI or trap semantics.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-003-r8s2 (broken, n/a): A tiny return stack only helps if its speculative fetch-side target stays synchronized with the architectural pipeline; otherwise canonical JALR return hints can desynchronize `pc_fwd` even when the predictor is return-only.\n- 2026-05-16 hyp-20260516-002-r9s1 (regression, -7.54%): Hoisting one-hot instruction classes only pays when it removes enough work from the hot decode path; if ID still rechecks `funct3`/`funct7` and the wider predecode bundle adds fanout, the extra metadata can slow CoreMark instead of helping.","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/LESSONS.md","time":{"start":1778933647288,"end":1778933647593}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0b8645a0eb9a8243016a085f8f1984819bbf6f09247585d9c0"}},"id":"prt_e30b5479e001nfqHP2H25K9f7X","sessionID":"ses_1cf4ad38fffet6KoTg5NSa2pJh","messageID":"msg_e30b53214001zLXHPUE8Tf3h1c"}}
