{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778919411617,"sessionID":"ses_1d0264583ffe1bCdEqro7y3me0","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_fmR2msVeoAmMSl4IwWRXThYW","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r8s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-003-r8s2\n+title: \"Return-only stack predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still pays a repeatable bubble on function returns, and the prior\n+  broader predictor attempts were too expensive in IF. A very small return\n+  stack can target that hot path without adding full BTB logic or changing\n+  the architectural redirect source of truth.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny return-address stack that predicts only canonical JALR returns.\n+  Update it from already-latched PC+4 on resolved calls, use it speculatively\n+  only when the fetched instruction decodes as a return, and keep EX-stage\n+  redirection authoritative for recovery. By narrowing the predictor to\n+  returns only, the design should stay much cheaper than the earlier\n+  call/return predictor while still removing recurring control-flow bubbles.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Use the return-stack hint for speculative next-PC selection on canonical returns, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire resolved call/return metadata and PC+4 into the predictor update path without changing RVFI or trap behavior.\"\n+  - file: rtl/return_stack.sv\n+    description: \"Add a tiny return-address stack and canonical return-classification logic.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r8s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hyp