{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778925104381,"sessionID":"ses_1cfcdbfbcffeKpqDD4Zh6MbCCG","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_pRZDEDRChyzPmXFVYJPTmPya","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260516-003-r9s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260516-003-r9s2\n+title: \"Tiny bimodal branch predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\n+  conditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\n+  and stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\n+  so a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a tiny direct-mapped bimodal predictor that stores only 2-bit taken\n+  confidence for conditional branches, with the existing IF-stage immediate\n+  decode used to choose between fall-through and the predicted branch target.\n+  Update the table only from retired conditional branches, keep EX-stage\n+  resolution authoritative for recovery, and avoid BTB or return-stack\n+  target storage entirely so the predictor stays cheap enough for this FPGA.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs the decoded branch target before EX resolves the branch.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"\n+  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n+    description: \"Add a tiny direct-mapped 2-bit branch-direction table with conservative fall-through defaults and retirement-time updates.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hyp