stage.sv\n27:     description: \"Latch the source-read metadata with the fetched instruction so downstream logic can use it without re-decoding.\"\n28:   - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n29:     description: \"Gate the load-use compare tree with the source-read metadata so unused source fields cannot trigger false stalls.\"\n30:   - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n31:     description: \"Use the source-read metadata to prune unnecessary EX/MEM and MEM/WB source comparisons.\"\n32:   - file: rtl/core.sv\n33:     description: \"Wire the new source-read metadata from fetch into hazard and forwarding without changing the RVFI contract.\"\n\n(End of file - total 33 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"id: hyp-20260517-001-r14s0\ntitle: \"Operand-read compare pruning\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  CoreMark mixes many immediate and single-source instructions with a small\n  number of true register-register dependencies. The current load-use and\n  forwarding compares still look at both source fields on every cycle, which\n  adds always-on fanout and can create false stalls on instructions that do\n  not architecturally read rs2.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits to the fetch-time control bundle,\n  derived from the same validated opcode/funct classification the decoder\n  already uses, and route those bits into both the load-use interlock and the\n  EX-stage forwarding compares. This should prune irrelevant compare logic\n  for instructions that do not read a source register while keeping all\n  ambiguous cases pessimistic so RVFI, traps, and retirement order stay the\n  same.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-001-r14s0.yaml","time":{"start":1778991396599,"end":1778991396604}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_066a484ba280daf2016a09412464e48195a6174b296ba4225c"}},"id":"prt_e342676f5002puNF1zudPFiNxT","sessionID":"ses_1cbdaa83