{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778990731803,"sessionID":"ses_1cbe55ca0ffe5uO6odBkwh0T7L","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_0XT3JVzYnfCKgWJ0hhbu3Mew","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r13s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-002-r13s1\n+title: \"Split writeback from RVFI\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The current post-MEM boundary still carries RVFI-only state through the same\n+  bundle used by the regfile and bypass network. That makes the hottest retire\n+  path wider than it needs to be on every instruction, even though the\n+  forwarding logic only needs a narrow writeback view.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Split the MEM/WB interface into a narrow writeback bundle and a separate\n+  retirement snapshot. Keep rd/rd_wdata/valid/reg_write on the path used by\n+  the regfile and forwarding network, and move RVFI-only fields onto a sideband\n+  that is consumed only at the core boundary. This should reduce fanout and\n+  routing pressure on the always-on post-MEM path without changing retirement\n+  semantics.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n+    description: \"Define a narrow writeback bundle plus a separate retire snapshot so post-MEM state can be split cleanly.\"\n+  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Latch the narrow writeback data independently from the RVFI snapshot at the MEM/WB boundary.\"\n+  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n+    description: \"Compare against the narrow writeback metadata instead of the full retirement snapshot.\"\n+  - file: rtl/wb_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Drive the regfile from the narrow writeback bundle with no dependency on RVFI-only fields.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire RVFI from the separate retirement snapshot while keeping bypass and regfile traffic on the narrow path.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. 