{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778991456350,"sessionID":"ses_1cbdaa750ffeEKojB10d0Fz4Y7","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_wK2d6uJa7iBDlNqDx6XL3diU","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-002-r14s1\n+title: \"Replicate regfile read banks\"\n+category: structural\n+motivation: |\n+  The ID stage hits the register file on every instruction, and the shared\n+  read-side structure still fans the same storage and bypass compare logic into\n+  both operand ports. Duplicating the read path should shorten that always-on\n+  operand cone and give Fmax headroom without changing architectural state.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Rework `reg_file.sv` into two read-port-private copies of the 32x32 register\n+  array, with one broadcast write port keeping both copies coherent and the\n+  existing same-cycle write-first behavior preserved per port. This is a\n+  structural timing play: each read port gets a smaller, more local mux tree,\n+  reducing fanout on the hottest decode-to-execute operand path while leaving\n+  visible register semantics unchanged.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n+    description: \"Split the register storage into two read-port-specific copies and keep x0, write-first bypass, and write coherence behavior identical.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-002-r14s1.yaml\n+++ /Users/bone