{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778990011484,"sessionID":"ses_1cbef4b2effeqkt5iwsBm9acoY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_inCt7MYKy4WrQioDY4a3s7Io","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r12s2.yaml\n+id: hyp-20260517-003-r12s2\n+title: \"Retired loop-count predictor\"\n+category: predictor\n+motivation: |\n+  CoreMark still burns cycles on the same short loop backedges, but the\n+  earlier broader predictor attempts were either too expensive in IF or too\n+  speculative in their state updates. A tiny predictor that learns one hot\n+  loop at a time from retired branches is the safest remaining way to hide\n+  fetch bubbles without changing the architectural redirect contract.\n+hypothesis: |\n+  Add a single-entry loop-count predictor that remembers the most recent\n+  retired backward conditional branch PC, its target, and a small trip-count\n+  confidence counter. Speculate only on exact-PC hits for that loop, train\n+  only from retired branch outcomes, and always let EX-stage redirect remain\n+  the source of truth for recovery so the predictor stays narrow and\n+  CoreMark-focused.\n+expected_impact:\n+  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n+  confidence: medium\n+changes:\n+  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n+    description: \"Use the loop predictor to choose the speculative next PC for exact-hit backward branches while preserving EX-stage redirect priority.\"\n+  - file: rtl/core.sv\n+    description: \"Wire retired branch PC, target, and taken/not-taken outcome into the predictor update path without changing RVFI behavior.\"\n+  - file: rtl/branch_predictor.sv\n+    description: \"Add a one-entry loop-count predictor with exact-PC tagging, cached target, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nA cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260517-003-r12s2.yaml","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/bo