{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778990646564,"sessionID":"ses_1cbe55b85ffeSfXCysNCLpVqZc","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_VZoEfs8tXmhiLbItCeQekYTy","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl","offset":28,"limit":12},"output":"<path>/Users/bonetto/bonetto/auto-arch-tournament/.claude/bench-runs/gpt-5_4-mini-rep2/cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n28: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny bimodal branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\\nconditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\\nand stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\\nso a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\\nconditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\\nand stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\\nso a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped bimodal predictor that stores only 2-bit taken\\nconfidence for conditional branches, with the existing IF-stage immediate\\ndecode used to choose between fall-through and the predicted branch target.\\nUpdate the table only from retired conditional branches, keep EX-stage\\nresolution authoritative for recovery, and avoid BTB or return-stack\\ntarget storage entirely so the predictor stays cheap enough for this FPGA.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs the decoded branch target before EX resolves the branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped 2-bit branch-direction table with conservative fall-through defaults and retirement-time updates.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n29: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Decoder-aligned stall masks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource registers. The current load-use interlock still compares raw rs\\nfields unconditionally, so it burns fanout and can inject false bubbles\\non common immediate and U-type instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource registers. The current load-use interlock still compares raw rs\\nfields unconditionally, so it burns fanout and can inject false bubbles\\non common immediate and U-type instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` metadata to the fetch-time predecode\\nbundle, derived from the same opcode-class rules the decoder uses, and\\nuse those bits to gate the load-use compare tree in `hazard_unit.sv`.\\nThis should remove avoidable stall checks on instructions that do not\\narchitecturally read rs2, while keeping RVFI, traps, and forwarding\\nbehavior unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` to the predecode bundle so source-read intent can ride with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch exact source-operand-use bits alongside the existing predecode fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate the load-use compare logic with the source-read bits so unused rs fields cannot trigger false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` to the predecode bundle so source-read intent can ride with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch exact source-operand-use bits alongside the existing predecode fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n30: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split retire and writeback\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB boundary carries a lot of RVFI-only state through the\\nsame bundle that feeds the regfile and forwarding logic. That makes the\\nhottest post-MEM path wider than it needs to be.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB boundary carries a lot of RVFI-only state through the\\nsame bundle that feeds the regfile and forwarding logic. That makes the\\nhottest post-MEM path wider than it needs to be.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the wide retirement snapshot from the narrow writeback/bypass path:\\nkeep only rd, rd_wdata, reg_write, and valid on the signals used by the\\nregfile and forwarding network, and carry the full RVFI snapshot on a\\nseparate sideband bundle. This should reduce fanout and routing pressure on\\nthe MEM/WB boundary without changing architectural retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define separate narrow writeback and full retire-snapshot bundle types so the post-MEM path can be split cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the narrow writeback data separately from the full RVFI retirement snapshot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrow writeback metadata instead of the full MEM/WB bundle for bypass comparisons.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive the regfile from the narrow writeback bundle with no dependency on RVFI-only fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the split bundles so RVFI comes from the retire snapshot while writeback and forwarding use the narrow path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define separate narrow writeback and full retire-snapshot bundle types so the post-MEM path can be split cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"r... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n31: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Narrow loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\\nor formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\\nrecent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\\nwhile still removing a common fetch bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\\nor formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\\nrecent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\\nwhile still removing a common fetch bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny one-entry loop-backedge predictor in IF that remembers the last\\ntaken conditional branch PC, its target, and a single taken bit. Speculate\\nonly on exact PC hits for backward conditional branches, update only from\\nretired conditional branches, and keep EX-stage redirect authoritative for\\nrecovery. This narrows the predictor enough to avoid the fanout and control\\ncomplexity that hurt earlier predictor ideas while still targeting CoreMark's\\ndominant loop pattern.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the loop-backedge hint to choose the speculative next PC on exact-hit backward branches, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor with one-bit taken state, exact PC tagging, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"conf... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n32: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Cache pc+4 in IF\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The sequential fall-through PC is needed on every instruction, but the\\ncurrent core still recomputes `pc + 4` in ID. Hoisting that universal add\\ninto fetch should trim a small amount of always-on decode-path logic and\\nreduce fanout through the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The sequential fall-through PC is needed on every instruction, but the\\ncurrent core still recomputes `pc + 4` in ID. Hoisting that universal add\\ninto fetch should trim a small amount of always-on decode-path logic and\\nreduce fanout through the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a cached `pc_plus4` field to the IF/ID bundle, compute it once in\\n`if_stage.sv`, and have `id_stage.sv` latch that value directly instead of\\nrebuilding `pc + 4` every cycle. This keeps retirement, traps, and RVFI\\nbehavior unchanged while moving a universal 32-bit add out of the ID hot\\npath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `pc_plus4` to the IF/ID bundle so fetch can carry the sequential PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch `pc_plus4` alongside the fetched instruction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the cached sequential PC instead of recomputing `pc + 4` in decode.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `pc_plus4` to the IF/ID bundle so fetch can carry the sequential PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch `pc_plus4` alongside the fetched instruction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the cached sequential PC instead of recomputing `pc + 4` in decode.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.99, \"delta_pct\": -9.25, \"vs_baseline\": 9.9... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n33: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist JALR target\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot control-flow work in EX is the JALR rs1+imm add and\\nits misalignment bookkeeping. CoreMark still exercises call/return paths\\nenough that moving that arithmetic earlier should trim the execute-side\\ntiming cone without changing the architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot control-flow work in EX is the JALR rs1+imm add and\\nits misalignment bookkeeping. CoreMark still exercises call/return paths\\nenough that moving that arithmetic earlier should trim the execute-side\\ntiming cone without changing the architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow ID-side JALR precompute path: forward the rs1 operand into\\ndecode only for JALR, latch the resulting `jalr_target` in the ID/EX\\nbundle, and have EX reuse that stored target for redirect and\\nmisalignment checks. Keep EX as the architectural resolve point, but\\nremove the live `rs1 + imm` adder from the critical execute path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched `jalr_target` field for JALR redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a minimal JALR-specific rs1 forwarding path that decode can use to compute the target early.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the JALR target during decode using the forwarded rs1 operand.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched JALR target instead of rebuilding `rs1 + imm` in EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new ID-side JALR target precompute path through the top-level pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched `jalr_target` field for JALR redi... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n34: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Retired loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still burns cycles on the same hot loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts paid too much in IF logic or speculative\\nstate. A tiny predictor anchored to retired control-flow state is the\\nnarrowest remaining way to hide those fetch bubbles without changing\\nthe architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still burns cycles on the same hot loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts paid too much in IF logic or speculative\\nstate. A tiny predictor anchored to retired control-flow state is the\\nnarrowest remaining way to hide those fetch bubbles without changing\\nthe architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor that remembers the most\\nrecent retired taken conditional branch PC and target, then speculates\\nonly on exact PC hits for backward branches. Train it strictly from\\nretired branch outcomes, keep EX-stage redirect authoritative for every\\nrecovery path, and avoid any extra speculative training state so the\\npredictor stays small enough to help CoreMark without dragging Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume a tiny loop-backedge hint for speculative next-PC selection, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing RVFI or trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor with exact-PC tagging, a cached target, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume a tiny loop-backedge hint for speculative next-PC ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n35: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Exact source-read stall masks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both raw source fields on every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read\\none of them. That adds always-on compare fanout and can also create\\nfalse bubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\nA conservative source-read mask should trim that hot hazard path without\\nchanging retirement behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both raw source fields on every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read\\none of them. That adds always-on compare fanout and can also create\\nfalse bubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\nA conservative source-read mask should trim that hot hazard path without\\nchanging retirement behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits to the fetch-time predecode\\nbundle, derived from the same opcode/funct classification the decoder\\nalready trusts. Then gate the load-use compare tree in `hazard_unit.sv`\\nwith those bits so it only checks source registers the current\\ninstruction definitely reads. Keep ambiguous cases pessimistic, so any\\nuncertain opcode still stalls rather than risking an architectural miss.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add conservative `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` fields to the predecode bundle so source-read intent rides with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the exact source-read masks alongside the existing opcode/funct predecode.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the source-read masks to skip irrelevant load-use compares and avoid false stalls on immediate-style instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the predecoded sour... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n36: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist full decode into fetch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"ID still rebuilds the validated control bundle from the raw instruction on every cycle.\\nSince fetch already owns the instruction slice, hoisting decode one stage earlier should\\nshorten the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path and reduce instruction fanout without changing\\nretirement, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"ID still rebuilds the validated control bundle from the raw instruction on every cycle.\\nSince fetch already owns the instruction slice, hoisting decode one stage earlier should\\nshorten the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path and reduce instruction fanout without changing\\nretirement, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the IF/ID bundle with a fully decoded control record, instantiate the existing\\ndecoder at fetch time, and latch that control alongside the predecoded instruction\\nmetadata. Then make ID a narrower register-read stage that consumes the latched control\\nbundle instead of rebuilding opcode/funct legality and control signals locally. This keeps\\nthe ISA contract identical while removing the decoder case tree from the ID critical path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the IF/ID bundle with a latched control record so fetch can hand decode results forward with the instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Decode each fetched instruction once at fetch time and carry the validated control bundle forward with the predecoded metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched control bundle and remove the local decoder/control-tree work from the hot ID path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the IF/ID bundle with a latched control record so fetch can hand decode results fo... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n37: {\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-003-r12s2\", \"title\": \"Retired loop-count predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still burns cycles on the same short loop backedges, but the\\nearlier broader predictor attempts were either too expensive in IF or too\\nspeculative in their state updates. A tiny predictor that learns one hot\\nloop at a time from retired branches is the safest remaining way to hide\\nfetch bubbles without changing the architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still burns cycles on the same short loop backedges, but the\\nearlier broader predictor attempts were either too expensive in IF or too\\nspeculative in their state updates. A tiny predictor that learns one hot\\nloop at a time from retired branches is the safest remaining way to hide\\nfetch bubbles without changing the architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry loop-count predictor that remembers the most recent\\nretired backward conditional branch PC, its target, and a small trip-count\\nconfidence counter. Speculate only on exact-PC hits for that loop, train\\nonly from retired branch outcomes, and always let EX-stage redirect remain\\nthe source of truth for recovery so the predictor stays narrow and\\nCoreMark-focused.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the loop predictor to choose the speculative next PC for exact-hit backward branches while preserving EX-stage redirect priority.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired branch PC, target, and taken/not-taken outcome into the predictor update path without changing RVFI behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a one-entry loop-count predictor with exact-PC tagging, cached target, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\"... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(End of file - total 37 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r9s2\", \"title\": \"Tiny bimodal branch predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\\nconditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\\nand stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\\nso a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a meaningful fraction of its time on the same\\nconditional backedges and short control-flow loops. The earlier static\\nand stack-based predictor ideas were either too expensive or too broad,\\nso a smaller learned direction predictor is the best remaining angle.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped bimodal predictor that stores only 2-bit taken\\nconfidence for conditional branches, with the existing IF-stage immediate\\ndecode used to choose between fall-through and the predicted branch target.\\nUpdate the table only from retired conditional branches, keep EX-stage\\nresolution authoritative for recovery, and avoid BTB or return-stack\\ntarget storage entirely so the predictor stays cheap enough for this FPGA.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs the decoded branch target before EX resolves the branch.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a tiny direct-mapped 2-bit branch-direction table with conservative fall-through defaults and retirement-time updates.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use a small bimodal direction predictor to speculatively choose fall-through vs... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r10s0\", \"title\": \"Decoder-aligned stall masks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource registers. The current load-use interlock still compares raw rs\\nfields unconditionally, so it burns fanout and can inject false bubbles\\non common immediate and U-type instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark spends a lot of time on instructions that do not read both\\nsource registers. The current load-use interlock still compares raw rs\\nfields unconditionally, so it burns fanout and can inject false bubbles\\non common immediate and U-type instructions.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` metadata to the fetch-time predecode\\nbundle, derived from the same opcode-class rules the decoder uses, and\\nuse those bits to gate the load-use compare tree in `hazard_unit.sv`.\\nThis should remove avoidable stall checks on instructions that do not\\narchitecturally read rs2, while keeping RVFI, traps, and forwarding\\nbehavior unchanged.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` to the predecode bundle so source-read intent can ride with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch exact source-operand-use bits alongside the existing predecode fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Gate the load-use compare logic with the source-read bits so unused rs fields cannot trigger false stalls.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` to the predecode bundle so source-read intent can ride with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch exact source-operand-use bits alongside the existing predecode fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r10s1\", \"title\": \"Split retire and writeback\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB boundary carries a lot of RVFI-only state through the\\nsame bundle that feeds the regfile and forwarding logic. That makes the\\nhottest post-MEM path wider than it needs to be.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The current MEM/WB boundary carries a lot of RVFI-only state through the\\nsame bundle that feeds the regfile and forwarding logic. That makes the\\nhottest post-MEM path wider than it needs to be.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Split the wide retirement snapshot from the narrow writeback/bypass path:\\nkeep only rd, rd_wdata, reg_write, and valid on the signals used by the\\nregfile and forwarding network, and carry the full RVFI snapshot on a\\nseparate sideband bundle. This should reduce fanout and routing pressure on\\nthe MEM/WB boundary without changing architectural retirement behavior.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define separate narrow writeback and full retire-snapshot bundle types so the post-MEM path can be split cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/mem_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Latch the narrow writeback data separately from the full RVFI retirement snapshot.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the narrow writeback metadata instead of the full MEM/WB bundle for bypass comparisons.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/wb_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Drive the regfile from the narrow writeback bundle with no dependency on RVFI-only fields.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the split bundles so RVFI comes from the retire snapshot while writeback and forwarding use the narrow path.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Define separate narrow writeback and full retire-snapshot bundle types so the post-MEM path can be split cleanly.\"}, {\"file\": \"r... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r10s2\", \"title\": \"Narrow loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\\nor formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\\nrecent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\\nwhile still removing a common fetch bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still spends a lot of time on the same loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts so far have paid too much in IF logic, state,\\nor formal complexity. A very small predictor that only tracks the most\\nrecent conditional backedge should be cheaper than a BTB or return stack\\nwhile still removing a common fetch bubble.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a tiny one-entry loop-backedge predictor in IF that remembers the last\\ntaken conditional branch PC, its target, and a single taken bit. Speculate\\nonly on exact PC hits for backward conditional branches, update only from\\nretired conditional branches, and keep EX-stage redirect authoritative for\\nrecovery. This narrows the predictor enough to avoid the fanout and control\\ncomplexity that hurt earlier predictor ideas while still targeting CoreMark's\\ndominant loop pattern.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the loop-backedge hint to choose the speculative next PC on exact-hit backward branches, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing the RVFI or trap contract.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor with one-bit taken state, exact PC tagging, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"conf... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-001-r11s0\", \"title\": \"Cache pc+4 in IF\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The sequential fall-through PC is needed on every instruction, but the\\ncurrent core still recomputes `pc + 4` in ID. Hoisting that universal add\\ninto fetch should trim a small amount of always-on decode-path logic and\\nreduce fanout through the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The sequential fall-through PC is needed on every instruction, but the\\ncurrent core still recomputes `pc + 4` in ID. Hoisting that universal add\\ninto fetch should trim a small amount of always-on decode-path logic and\\nreduce fanout through the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX boundary.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a cached `pc_plus4` field to the IF/ID bundle, compute it once in\\n`if_stage.sv`, and have `id_stage.sv` latch that value directly instead of\\nrebuilding `pc + 4` every cycle. This keeps retirement, traps, and RVFI\\nbehavior unchanged while moving a universal 32-bit add out of the ID hot\\npath.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `pc_plus4` to the IF/ID bundle so fetch can carry the sequential PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch `pc_plus4` alongside the fetched instruction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the cached sequential PC instead of recomputing `pc + 4` in decode.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 3, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add `pc_plus4` to the IF/ID bundle so fetch can carry the sequential PC forward.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch `pc_plus4` alongside the fetched instruction metadata.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the cached sequential PC instead of recomputing `pc + 4` in decode.\"}], \"outcome\": \"regression\", \"fitness\": 310.99, \"delta_pct\": -9.25, \"vs_baseline\": 9.9... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-002-r11s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist JALR target\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The remaining hot control-flow work in EX is the JALR rs1+imm add and\\nits misalignment bookkeeping. CoreMark still exercises call/return paths\\nenough that moving that arithmetic earlier should trim the execute-side\\ntiming cone without changing the architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The remaining hot control-flow work in EX is the JALR rs1+imm add and\\nits misalignment bookkeeping. CoreMark still exercises call/return paths\\nenough that moving that arithmetic earlier should trim the execute-side\\ntiming cone without changing the architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a narrow ID-side JALR precompute path: forward the rs1 operand into\\ndecode only for JALR, latch the resulting `jalr_target` in the ID/EX\\nbundle, and have EX reuse that stored target for redirect and\\nmisalignment checks. Keep EX as the architectural resolve point, but\\nremove the live `rs1 + imm` adder from the critical execute path.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched `jalr_target` field for JALR redirects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/forward_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Expose a minimal JALR-specific rs1 forwarding path that decode can use to compute the target early.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/id_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the JALR target during decode using the forwarded rs1 operand.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume the latched JALR target instead of rebuilding `rs1 + imm` in EX.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the new ID-side JALR target precompute path through the top-level pipeline.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Extend the ID/EX bundle with a latched `jalr_target` field for JALR redi... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260516-003-r11s2\", \"title\": \"Retired loop-backedge predictor\", \"category\": \"predictor\", \"motivation\": \"CoreMark still burns cycles on the same hot loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts paid too much in IF logic or speculative\\nstate. A tiny predictor anchored to retired control-flow state is the\\nnarrowest remaining way to hide those fetch bubbles without changing\\nthe architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"CoreMark still burns cycles on the same hot loop backedges, but the\\nbroader predictor attempts paid too much in IF logic or speculative\\nstate. A tiny predictor anchored to retired control-flow state is the\\nnarrowest remaining way to hide those fetch bubbles without changing\\nthe architectural redirect contract.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor that remembers the most\\nrecent retired taken conditional branch PC and target, then speculates\\nonly on exact PC hits for backward branches. Train it strictly from\\nretired branch outcomes, keep EX-stage redirect authoritative for every\\nrecovery path, and avoid any extra speculative training state so the\\npredictor stays small enough to help CoreMark without dragging Fmax.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume a tiny loop-backedge hint for speculative next-PC selection, but always yield to EX-stage redirect recovery.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire retired conditional-branch outcome information into the predictor update path without changing RVFI or trap behavior.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/branch_predictor.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a single-entry loop-backedge predictor with exact-PC tagging, a cached target, and conservative fall-through on miss.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 4, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Consume a tiny loop-backedge hint for speculative next-PC ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-001-r12s0\", \"title\": \"Exact source-read stall masks\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both raw source fields on every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read\\none of them. That adds always-on compare fanout and can also create\\nfalse bubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\nA conservative source-read mask should trim that hot hazard path without\\nchanging retirement behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The load-use interlock still compares both raw source fields on every\\ninstruction, even when the opcode class does not architecturally read\\none of them. That adds always-on compare fanout and can also create\\nfalse bubbles on common immediate and U-type instructions in CoreMark.\\nA conservative source-read mask should trim that hot hazard path without\\nchanging retirement behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Add exact `uses_rs1` and `uses_rs2` bits to the fetch-time predecode\\nbundle, derived from the same opcode/funct classification the decoder\\nalready trusts. Then gate the load-use compare tree in `hazard_unit.sv`\\nwith those bits so it only checks source registers the current\\ninstruction definitely reads. Keep ambiguous cases pessimistic, so any\\nuncertain opcode still stalls rather than risking an architectural miss.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 5, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/core_pkg.sv\", \"description\": \"Add conservative `uses_rs1` / `uses_rs2` fields to the predecode bundle so source-read intent rides with the fetched instruction.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/if_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Compute and latch the exact source-read masks alongside the existing opcode/funct predecode.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Use the source-read masks to skip irrelevant load-use compares and avoid false stalls on immediate-style instructions.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire the predecoded sour... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20260517-002-r12s1\", \"title\": \"Hoist full decode into fetch\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"ID still rebuilds the validated control bundle from the raw instruction on every cycle.\\nSince fetch already owns the instruction slice, hoisting decode one stage earlier should\\nshorten the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path and reduce instruction fanout without changing\\nretirement, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"ID still rebuilds the validated control bundle from the raw instruction on every cycle.\\nSince fetch already owns the instruction slice, hoisting decode one stage earlier should\\nshorten the hot IF/ID -> ID/EX path and reduce instruction fanout without changing\\nretirement, trap, or RVFI behavior.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Extend the IF/ID bundle with a fully decoded control record, instantiate the existing\\ndecoder at fetch time, and latch that control alongside the predecoded instru